;; §1 — THE SCIENTIFIC CLAIM
;; ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

(claim
  :name "isb-sft-composition-claim"
  :statement "Interleaved-Spaced-Bloom SFT (ISB-SFT) is a five-way composition of prior-art
              pedagogical mechanisms applied simultaneously to a densely-labeled corpus.
              Each mechanism exists in the literature; the composition on labeled substrate
              is uncommon because most researchers work with corpora they didn't build.
              Motoi is the first small (1.5B) LLM trained via this five-way composition
              on labeled substrate to our knowledge (2026-07-21)."

  :prior-art
  ("Bloom's Taxonomy — Bloom 1956 — six cognitive operations"
   "Interleaving — Rohrer & Taylor 2007 — 25-40% retention gain over blocking"
   "Spaced repetition — Ebbinghaus 1885, Bjork 1994 — expanding intervals"
   "Testing effect — Roediger & Karpicke 2006 — retrieval > exposure"
   "Instruction diversity — Chung et al. Flan-T5 2022 — task diversity > per-task volume"
   "Physics of Language Models 3.1 — Allen-Zhu 2023 — 5+ shapes per fact ~= 96% extraction"
   "Cognitive load / worked examples — Sweller 1988 — faded scaffolding"
   "Zone of Proximal Development — Vygotsky 1978 — slightly-above-current with scaffolds"
   "Deliberate practice — Ericsson 1993 — targeted work with feedback")

  :novelty-of-composition
  "The five composition elements:
     (1) Bloom's 6-ops per central concept
     (2) Philosopher-shape overlay tags on all pairs
     (3) Graph-cross-axis-bridge weighting
     (4) Pre-authored :short-scenario + :antipattern-scenario per principle
     (5) Intentional centrality-Zipfian oversampling

   Each alone is prior art. Composed simultaneously requires a substrate that labels
   all five aspects. Alfred's labeling investment in the Motoi corpus (16 philosopher
   books + 4 axis graphs + book-types + 5-row reference + graph :short/:antipattern
   scenarios + tf-idf/bridge-score metadata) is what makes the composition possible.
   Substrate-first, then composition.")


