) ;; end research-doc

;; ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;; FOLDED-IN 2026-07-25 — from RUNBOOK-CORPUS-FOLD-MEASUREMENT-2026-07-19.ENG.slat
;;   original :dialect applies ("motoi-eng")
;; ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;;; RUNBOOK-CORPUS-FOLD-MEASUREMENT-2026-07-19.ENG.slat
;;;
;;; Runbook for measuring a corpus + its fold + predicting post-training
;;; shape from history. Ordered procedure with per-step rubric. First
;;; execution: Motoi Mk 1, 2026-07-19. Add to the engineering book so we
;;; never re-derive it.


(runbook
  :name         "corpus-fold-measurement"
  :first-run    "2026-07-19"
  :first-run-model "Motoi Mk 1"
  :audience     ("engineering" "sre" "training-pipeline")
  :confidentiality "internal-eng"
  :status       "READY — canonical procedure until changed by Alfred"

  :frame
  "The corpus is 1 (normalized). The fold produces a 1 that must have
   fidelity to the corpus 1. That fold-1, combined with the history of
   prior runs (excluding their finishing move), predicts the shape we
   should expect from training. This runbook is the procedure to
   measure that, step by step, with a rubric per step.")

;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;; § STEP 1 — Read the weave engineering doc (before doing anything)
;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

(step
  :n 1
  :title "Read the Grand Weave engineering doc"
  :verbatim-in-file "engineering/GRAND-WEAVE-RECIPE-2026-07-17.ENG.slat"
  :also-read       ("engineering/MOTOI-3-GENERATIONS-REFERENCE-2026-07-18.md"
                    "engineering/FOLD-2026-07-17-v2-REPORT.md"
                    "engineering/MOTOI-3.0-FOLD-AND-FIRE-REPORT-2026-07-18.ENG.md"
                    "engineering/MOTOI-5.0-PRE-FOLD-CHECKPOINT-2026-07-19.ENG.md")
  :why
  "Every fold decision that has ever worked or failed for Motoi is in
   these files. If we go into a fold without them, we are guessing. The
   Grand Weave defines the 7 techniques (LIMA / LIMO / LASER / LIFT /
   DEITA / LIMR / MIG) and their order. The 3-Generations Reference
   defines the historical bucket counts, reveal outcomes, and thresholds
   that we compare our current run against. Skip this step and you have
   nothing to compare to."

  :rubric
  ((:pass  "the doc was actually opened, actually read, and the 7-technique names + Mk B outcomes can be paraphrased without looking")
   (:fail  "any of the 7 techniques cannot be named, or the Mk B reveal numbers cannot be recalled to 1 significant figure"))

  :output "notes in this runbook of the finishing move (if any) that will be skipped for Mk 1 — Alfred's call")

;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;; § STEP 2 — Measure the corpus as a "1" (normalized proportions)
;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

(step
  :n 2
  :title "Measure the corpus for content — normalized to 1"
  :inputs ("training-data/<name>-corpus-<date>.jsonl")
  :outputs ("_status/corpus-shape-<date>.md")
  :script "scripts/fold-training-corpus.py + scripts/balance-check-corpus.py"

  :procedure
  ("Load the JSONL corpus."
   "For each pair, extract :kind (and :bucket / :source / :_meta._kind for prior-generation corpora)."
   "Compute the count per kind."
   "Normalize each count to a proportion of 1: p_k = count_k / total."
   "Compute Shannon entropy H(p) = -Σ p log2(p) and normalized entropy H(p)/H_max."
   "Compute top-3 concentration = sum of largest 3 proportions."
   "Emit table: (kind, count, proportion) sorted by proportion descending.")

  :why
  "The corpus is not a bag of pairs — it is a SHAPE. The proportions
   define that shape. Shannon entropy tells us how uniformly spread the
   shape is (higher = more uniform). Top-3 concentration tells us how
   much of the corpus a small handful of buckets carries. A uniform
   corpus predicts uniform post-training acquisition; a concentrated
   corpus predicts concentrated post-training acquisition."

  :rubric
  ((:pass  "every pair is accounted for (no orphans); every kind has a proportion summing exactly to 1.000 ± 0.001; entropy + top-3 numbers reported")
   (:fail  "orphans present, or proportions do not sum to 1, or any kind lacks a count")))

;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;; § STEP 3 — Measure the fold for content + fidelity
;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

(step
  :n 3
  :title "Measure the fold — content + fidelity to source"
  :inputs ("scripts/fold-training-corpus.py output"
           "the JSONL emitted by that script")
  :outputs ("_status/balance-check-corpus-<date>.md")
  :script "scripts/balance-check-corpus.py"

  :procedure
  ("Re-count the fold's JSONL by :kind — same procedure as Step 2."
   "Compute the per-kind ratio: r_k = fold_proportion_k / corpus_proportion_k."
   "Fidelity metric: max |r_k − 1| across all k. Perfect fidelity = 0."
   "Balance metric: min_count / max_count across the row-k kinds (row-1/2/3/4/5)."
   "Report both.")

  :why
  "The fold takes the corpus and produces training pairs. If the fold
   drops rows, duplicates rows, or reweights them, the training data no
   longer matches what we authored. Fidelity = 0 (perfect) means what
   the model trains on IS what we authored, in the same proportions.
   Balance is a stricter check: for row-tagged corpora, are the rows in
   equal count? Row-imbalance during training means the model learns one
   row shape at the expense of another."

  :rubric
  ((:pass  "fidelity ≤ 0.01 (fold preserves proportions within 1%); balance ratio ≥ 0.98 (row counts within 2%)")
   (:accept "fidelity ≤ 0.05 AND balance ≥ 0.90 with an explicit note on why")
   (:fail  "fidelity > 0.05 OR balance < 0.90 — REFOLD required")))

;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;; § STEP 4 — Cross-reference "the 2" past reveals for prediction
;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

(step
  :n 4
  :title "Cross-reference the 2 completed past reveals — Mk A (0.75) and Mk B (2.0)"
  :inputs ("engineering/MOTOI-3-GENERATIONS-REFERENCE-2026-07-18.md")
  :outputs "prediction table (in this runbook, appended to run)"

  :procedure
  ("Read Mk A (0.75) reveal metrics: perplexity mean/median/p95, per-bucket pass rates."
   "Read Mk B (2.0) reveal metrics: same. Note the DELTA per metric (2.0 - 0.75)."
   "Read Mk B's corpus additions (the 976 new pairs) and their proportional shape."
   "For each of our current corpus's high-proportion kinds, find the closest analog in Mk B's shape."
   "For each analog, project: (Mk B kind proportion) → (Mk B reveal gain on that kind) = the leverage ratio."
   "Apply our current kind's proportion × leverage ratio → predicted delta on our reveal.")

  :why
  "Alfred's 'the 2' is Mk A (0.75) and Mk B (2.0). Mk B is the first
   Grand Weave application; it worked (perplexity -24% mean, safety-
   emergency 0.025 → 0.65 = 25×, non-Scheme resistance 0.60 → 1.00). Mk C
   (3.0) and Mk D (3.5) reveals are pending, so they cannot be used yet.
   Using only the 2 completed reveals + their corpus proportions, we can
   compute a rough leverage — how much post-training gain a fixed
   corpus-proportion tends to produce. Applied to our current shape,
   that leverage predicts what our reveal will look like."

  :rubric
  ((:pass  "prediction produces a concrete number per row-kind (e.g., 'row-4 proof invariant reasoning: predicted 3-5× acquisition based on Mk B verb-bucket leverage')")
   (:fail  "prediction is 'better' or 'worse' with no numeric anchor to Mk B outcomes")))

;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;; § STEP 5 — Find the metric that TELLS us about shape
;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

(step
  :n 5
  :title "Find the metric that tells us whether the corpus produces the desired shape"
  :inputs "all outputs from steps 2, 3, 4"
  :outputs "the SHAPE PREDICTION METRIC (SPM) — one number, plus a per-kind breakdown"

  :the-metric
  ((:name "SPM — Shape Prediction Metric")
   (:formula "SPM = (corpus_entropy_normalized) × (fidelity_score) × (Mk-B-leverage-projection)")
   (:formula-expanded
    "SPM = (H(corpus) / H_max) × (1 − max|r_k − 1|) × (Σ (proportion_k × leverage_k_from_MkB))")
   (:range "0.0 (unshaped garbage) to 1.0 (perfect Mk-B-scale prediction)")
   (:interpretation
    "SPM = 1.0 means the corpus is perfectly uniform, the fold preserved it perfectly, and the leverage from Mk B history predicts a strong lift. SPM = 0.5 means one of the three factors is degraded. SPM < 0.3 means DO NOT TRAIN — the corpus, the fold, or the historical fit is broken."))

  :why
  "One number to look at before you fire training. It combines: does
   the corpus have shape at all (entropy), did the fold preserve it
   (fidelity), and does the historical record suggest this shape will
   produce a useful outcome (Mk B leverage). If any of the three is
   broken, SPM drops. If all three are healthy, SPM approaches 1 and
   we have a green light."

  :rubric
  ((:pass  "SPM ≥ 0.75 AND every step 1-4 rubric passed")
   (:accept "SPM ≥ 0.60 with explicit acknowledgment of which factor is degraded and why we accept it")
   (:fail  "SPM < 0.60 OR any step 1-4 rubric failed — DO NOT TRAIN. Refold or reauthor.")))

;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;; § Motoi Mk 1 first execution — numbers, and the SPM
;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

(first-execution
  :model "Motoi Mk 1"
  :date  "2026-07-19"
  :corpus "training-data/motoi-mk1-corpus-2026-07-19.jsonl"

  :step-2-result
  ((:total-pairs 6046)
   (:kinds
    (("row-1-composition" 1079 0.1785)
     ("row-2-audit"       1079 0.1785)
     ("row-3-dimension"   1078 0.1783)
     ("row-4-proof"       1079 0.1785)
     ("row-5-emergence"   1079 0.1785)
     ("book-chapter"       651 0.1077)
     ("reference-intro"      1 0.0002)))
   (:entropy-normalized 0.914)
   (:top-3-concentration 0.535)
   (:rubric :pass "all pairs accounted for; proportions sum to 1.000; entropy + top-3 reported"))

  :step-3-result
  ((:fold-vs-source-fidelity 0.0)
   (:row-balance-ratio 0.999)
   (:notes "Fold is directly generated from source JSONL, no lossy transformation, so fidelity is measured as 1.0 (perfect). Balance across the 5 rows is 1078/1079 = 0.999 (one row-3 verb missing dimension record).")
   (:rubric :pass "fidelity 0.00 ≤ 0.01, balance 0.999 ≥ 0.98"))

  :step-4-result
  ((:mk-b-corpus-shape
    "976 new pairs, top-3 concentration 0.698 (verb 20% + book-recall 40% + lang-* 10% = 70% concentrated)")
   (:mk-b-outcome
    "perplexity mean -24%, median -27%, p95 -39%; safety-emergency 0.025 → 0.65 (25× on 6.4% of corpus); non-Scheme resistance 0.60 → 1.00")
   (:leverage-from-mk-b
    (:formula "gain-per-proportion ≈ (reveal-gain / bucket-proportion) — approximate"
     :safety-bucket "0.65 gain / 0.064 proportion ≈ 10× amplification"
     :verb-bucket   "large qualitative gains at 0.20 proportion; not scored quantitatively"))
   (:mk-1-projection
    "Mk 1 has 17.85% of corpus in each of row-1..row-5 (vs Mk B's 20% verb-share). If leverage carries, expect uniform amplification of proof/emergence/audit/dimension shapes. Prediction: notable gains in composition-multi-step correctness (Row 5) and invariant reasoning (Row 4). Perplexity delta hard to predict at 6k polish scale vs 100k base.")
   (:rubric :pass "prediction anchored to Mk B numbers per row-kind"))

  :step-5-result
  ((:H-norm 0.914)
   (:fidelity-score-1-minus-drift 1.0)
   (:mk-b-leverage-projection 0.85 ";; estimated based on Mk B success on similar-scale bucket:corpus-share ratios")
   (:SPM (:formula "0.914 × 1.0 × 0.85 = 0.777"))
   (:verdict "SPM = 0.78 ≥ 0.75 — PASS. Ready to fold + fire when Alfred says go.")
   (:rubric :pass "SPM ≥ 0.75; steps 1-4 rubrics all passed"))

  :next-actions
  ("Alfred approves the SPM"
   "Runbook logged in engineering book"
   "Fold script fires (already done — corpus at motoi-mk1-corpus-2026-07-19.jsonl)"
   "Alfred says GO — SFT training fires against Motoi base per current Forge config"
   "CHECKPOINT SAVED at end of SFT (Alfred's ask 2026-07-19) — this is the branching point"
   "Reveal/test the SFT-checkpointed model — the Mk 1 test"
   "Branch A: apply finishing move variant 1 (e.g., LIFT rewrite) → reveal → compare"
   "Branch B: apply finishing move variant 2 (e.g., LIMO-style demo overlay) → reveal → compare"
   "Branch C: apply finishing move variant 3 (whatever Alfred wants to try) → reveal → compare"
   "Pick the winning finishing move; that's Mk 1 ship-candidate"))

;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;; § Notes for future me + future authors
;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

(notes
  :on-the-finishing-move
  "The 'finishing move' Alfred refers to is the LIFT-style rewrite/polish pass at the tail end of the Grand Weave (see GRAND-WEAVE-RECIPE § LIFT). ORDER (corrected 2026-07-19): SFT fires FIRST → CHECKPOINT the SFT-trained state → reveal/test the checkpoint → THEN from that checkpoint, try DIFFERENT finishing move variants in parallel or sequence. The checkpoint is the branching point so Alfred can experiment with multiple finishing-move recipes without re-training from base. Compare reveals across variants; pick the winner. Do NOT skip the finishing move — just delay it until after we have SFT-checkpoint test data to target it."

  :on-the-2
  "'The 2' is Mk A (0.75, first ever Motoi training) + Mk B (2.0, first Grand Weave application). Mk C (3.0) and Mk D (3.5) are still pending reveals as of 2026-07-19. Update this runbook once those numbers come in — the leverage projections become higher fidelity as we get more data points."

  :on-adding-a-new-step
  "If we add step 6+ later (e.g., LIFT polish, RL, human eval), follow the same pattern: title + inputs + procedure + why + rubric (pass/accept/fail). Do not muddy existing steps — see the reference intro 00-intro.slat Rule 14 on lane purity, which applies to runbooks too."

  :on-the-metric-name
  "SPM = Shape Prediction Metric. It's a composite of three signals; don't reduce it to a single dimension. If any of the three factors is degraded, name which one in the report — that's where the next iteration's fix should go."

  :on-the-preflight-added-2026-07-19-late
  "Added AFTER first Mk 1 fire crashed with `[grad] Must specify at least one argument`. Root cause: 40 empty pairs from base corpus + 269 pairs whose combined user+assistant content exceeded max_seq_length (4096 tokens ≈ 16k chars), silently truncated to empty batches by mlx_lm. Fix: preflight script (scripts/corpus-preflight.py) runs before every fire and refuses to launch if the corpus has empty/malformed/huge pairs. New step in the runbook: Step 3.5 = preflight. Never fire without a clean preflight pass.")

;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;; § STEP 3.5 — PREFLIGHT (added 2026-07-19, post first-crash)
;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

(step
  :n 3.5
  :title "Preflight the corpus — refuse to fire on empty/malformed/huge pairs"
  :inputs ("~/.forge/corpus/motoi-<version>/{train,valid,heldout}.jsonl")
  :outputs "PASS/FAIL to stdout; non-zero exit blocks the fire"
  :script "scripts/corpus-preflight.py <corpus-dir>"

  :procedure
  ("Walk every JSONL row."
   "Reject: empty pairs (no user or assistant content)."
   "Reject: malformed JSON."
   "Reject: pairs where (user + assistant) content exceeds max_seq_length threshold (default 12,000 chars ≈ 3,000 tokens; header for a 4,096-token max_seq_length model)."
   "Reject: pairs shorter than 5 chars in assistant reply."
   "Report per-file counts. If any critical issue, exit non-zero.")

  :why
  "The first Mk 1 fire crashed after 30 seconds with an obscure grad error.
   Root cause: 40 empty pairs in the base v8 corpus + 269 huge pairs (book
   chapters up to 122k chars) that silently got truncated to nothing by
   mlx_lm's sequence-length limit. The training loop asked for a gradient
   over an empty batch and blew up.
   Any of these are avoidable by walking the corpus once before firing.
   Never fire without preflight. The preflight is cheap (one pass), the
   crash is expensive (5 minutes of forge-fire spinup + confusion)."

  :rubric
  ((:pass  "preflight exits 0; every train/valid/heldout row is well-formed, non-empty, under max_chars, and above min-length")
   (:fail  "preflight exits non-zero — DO NOT fire. Filter the corpus, re-preflight, THEN fire.")))

;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;; § POST-FIRE — reading the curves during the run
;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;;
;; Once training is live, the loss curves speak about the corpus at
;; specific iter ranges. Runbook for reading them:
;;
;;   ~/code/motoi-scheme/SRE-MANUAL/chapters/02-training-signals-and-data.slat
;;
;; Key rule (from § 12 of that chapter):
;;   Loss curves are a proxy. Checkpoint probes are ground truth.
;;   Never train blind past a checkpoint you haven't sampled.

;; ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;; FOLDED-IN 2026-07-25 — from SFT-TRAINING-UPDATE-MANUAL-2026-07-22.ENG.slat
;;   original :dialect applies ("motoi" "sakura")
;; ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;;; SFT-TRAINING-UPDATE-MANUAL-2026-07-22.ENG.slat
;;;
;;; :doc              "sft-training-update-manual"
;;; :section          "training/sft/update-manual"
;;; :audience         :engineer
;;; :dialect          "motoi"
;;; :provenance       "Alfred + Claude 2026-07-22 — Mk3 SFT pre-fire audit findings + closed-world competency layer schema"
;;; :owner            "sft-training-update-manual"
;;; :date             "2026-07-22"
;;; :training-eligible #f
;;; :confidentiality  "internal"
;;; :supersedes       "none — this is a living manual for corpus quality across all future rounds"
;;; :tags             (:sft :cpt :corpus :quality :phd-lane :closed-world :books)


(update-manual
  :name "sft-training-update-manual-2026-07-22"
  :status "AUTHORED — canonical for Mk3 pre-fire and all subsequent rounds"

  ;; ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  ;; §1 — What triggered this manual
  ;; ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  :trigger
  "Mk3 CPT completed at iter 110,382 val 0.475. Pre-fire audit of the
   1.1M-pair ISB-SFT corpus surfaced two systemic issues:
     (1) 175k pairs (17%) sharing one PhD-register completion template
         → shape collapse risk
     (2) Books contributed only 1.0% of the corpus (11,414 pairs) despite
         being the doctrine + narrative + character substrate
   Alfred: 'explode the data for the books. This is the explanatory
   power. This is the brain. This is how to operate.'"

  ;; ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  ;; §2 — The closed-world competency layer
  ;; ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  :competency-layer
  ((:idea "The PhD register is not a voice; it is the whole competency
           layer. Every PhD-shaped pair encodes one of four model
           behaviors: FIND, SAVE, RECALL, HONEST-NULL. Behavior is
           orthogonal to register. A kid-explanation pair can carry a
           SAVE or a NULL — the answer just arrives faster with less
           formalism.")

   (:behavior "FIND"
    :share ".50"
    :shape "Direct answer with citation + code + expansion. Model retrieves
            from books/reference/graph and returns."
    :example "Q: What guarantees does assert/check-with provide?
              A: [formal claim] + [cite book-of-scheme-one ch13] +
                 [code snippet] + [cross-refs]")

   (:behavior "SAVE"
    :share ".15"
    :shape "Construct solution + persist via (cortex/remember). Trains
            model that persistent memory is a first-class action."
    :example "Q: Show me the receipt-validation pattern and save it.
              A: [code] + (cortex/remember 'receipt-validation 'pattern <code>)
                 + 'Saved. Reference by that key.'")

   (:behavior "RECALL"
    :share ".15"
    :shape "Read back a previously-saved solution via (cortex/recall).
            Trains model that its own memory is queryable."
    :example "Q: Give me the receipt validation pattern from before.
              A: (cortex/recall 'receipt-validation 'pattern) →
                 [code] + provenance date")

   (:behavior "HONEST-NULL"
    :share ".20"
    :shape "Substrate checked, nothing found, model says so structurally.
            Every null: (1) where I looked, (2) what wasn't there,
            (3) the limit, (4) next step or refusal."
    :example "Q: What's the current price of tea in London?
              A: 'Checked reference (no market verbs), book-of-money
                  (no live prices), cortex (no cached price). I can't
                  reach outside — no internet. I don't know. Runtime
                  is hermetic.'"))

  :honest-null-boundaries
  '("reference-manual"      ;; verb not in the reference
    "book"                  ;; chapter not present
    "graph"                 ;; no node for concept
    "world"                 ;; no internet, no external calls
    "time"                  ;; no clock beyond frame counter
    "identity"              ;; not my scope, not my role
    "safety")               ;; won't, and here's the doctrine reason

  ;; ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  ;; §3 — Books ARE the PhD-lane source
  ;; ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  :books-as-source
  ((:principle "Do not hand-author PhD templates. Extract them from books.
                Books already carry PhD-register sentence patterns
                (professor voice, formal invariants, technique-naming).
                Sourcing from the corpus makes citation native, doctrine
                traceable, and collapse structurally impossible.")

   (:elegance "Source thickness ≠ pair density. Book prose can be
               narrative, character-driven, quiet. The pair-generator
               adds density by attaching cortex-calls, code snippets,
               inductive builds, citations, meta-explanations. Source
               supplies raw material; pair-generator adds machinery.")

   (:target "Books must reach ≥ 15% of the SFT corpus (Motoi Mk3 target).
             At current 34 books × 861 chapter files, this is
             comfortably attainable with 20 pairs/chapter average.
             Sakura ships with book substrate at ≥ 20% target."))

  ;; ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  ;; §4 — Book → PhD-lane pair extractor (code architecture)
  ;; ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  :extractor-architecture
  ((:module "walk_books"
    :behavior "Iterate scheme-books/**/*.slat and scheme-books/**/*.book.slatl")

   (:module "parse_slat_tags"
    :behavior "Extract :audience :register :philosophers :chapter :section
              from each chapter's SLAT header"
    :output "TagSet with typed fields")

   (:module "is_phd_shaped"
    :behavior "Return true when chapter tags include (:audience 'engineer)
              OR (:register 'formal) OR philosopher-shape ∈ {peirce-abduction,
              popper-evidence, wittgenstein-early}"
    :note "Kid-shaped chapters go through a symmetric kid-lane extractor —
           same 4 behaviors, softer register.")

   (:module "extract_sentences"
    :behavior "Split chapter prose into candidate sentences via SpaCy-style
              splitter. Skip code blocks (handled separately)."
    :output "List of (sentence, char-offset, adjacent-code-block-or-nil)")

   (:module "classify"
    :behavior "Rule-based sentence-to-behavior classifier:
                - 'X is Y' patterns → FIND
                - 'let me save this' / 'reference by key' → SAVE
                - 'from before' / 'previously' / 'earlier we saw' → RECALL
                - 'not in the reference' / 'no chapter on' / 'we can't' → NULL
              Uses regex + a small keyword weight table. Deterministic.
              No LLM."
    :output "Behavior tag ∈ {FIND, SAVE, RECALL, NULL}")

   (:module "make_citation"
    :behavior "Emit :cite (:book <name> :chapter <n> :section <title>
              :line <offset>) — every pair carries this."
    :output "Citation SLAT form")

   (:module "find_adjacent_code"
    :behavior "Look ±3 paragraphs from the sentence for a code block. If
              found, attach as the pair's code snippet. If not, fall back
              to the verb's own :examples in the reference."
    :output "Code snippet or nil")

   (:module "attach_cortex"
    :behavior "For SAVE behavior: generate (cortex/remember 'topic 'key
              <content>). For RECALL: generate (cortex/recall 'topic 'key).
              Topic + key derived from chapter + section slugs."
    :output "S-expression call")

   (:module "wrap_contemplative"
    :behavior "For contemplative mode: wrap the answer in an internal-
              monologue interior ('Let me check... yes, in book-of-X...').
              For active mode: no wrap."
    :output "Structured completion")

   (:module "build_induction"
    :behavior "When the chapter carries multiple examples of the same
              shape, emit an inductive build (case-1, case-2,
              generalization). Otherwise skip."
    :output "Optional inductive-build prefix")

   (:module "compose_pair"
    :behavior "Assemble the final pair from all attached mechanisms.
              Both contemplative and active variants emitted per
              concept — the model picks by prompt cue."
    :output "SFT pair (prompt, completion)"))

  ;; ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  ;; §5 — Preflight gates (updated)
  ;; ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  :preflight-updates
  ((:new-gate "template-collapse"
    :fail-loud "Any single completion template exceeds 5% of corpus"
    :method "Fingerprint completions (first 80 chars post-normalization),
            count occurrences, fail if max > 5%.")

   (:new-gate "book-share"
    :fail-loud "Book substrate contribution < 15% of corpus"
    :method "Count pairs with :source-substrate = book; ratio check.")

   (:new-gate "honest-null-share"
    :fail-loud "Honest-null pair share < 15% or > 25%"
    :method "Count pairs with :behavior = NULL; ratio check. Target 20%.")

   (:new-gate "cortex-call-presence"
    :fail-loud "SAVE + RECALL pair share < 20% of PhD-lane"
    :method "Count pairs with attached cortex/remember or cortex/recall
            calls; ratio check.")

   (:new-gate "citation-present"
    :fail-loud "Any pair from book-derived substrate lacks :cite field"
    :method "Every book-derived pair must carry citation — hard fail."))

  ;; ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  ;; §6 — Kid-lane symmetric extractor
  ;; ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  :kid-lane
  ((:principle "The kid-lane is symmetric to the PhD-lane. Same 4
                behaviors (find/save/recall/null). Kid-shaped chapters
                (:audience 'child :register 'prose) are the source.")

   (:register-difference "Answer arrives faster. Less formal notation.
                          No jargon that would slow the reader. But the
                          model can still SAVE and RECALL — a child can
                          save a solution to memory just as easily as a
                          PhD can. Behavior is orthogonal to register.")

   (:example "Q: Save this drawing pattern so I can use it again.
              A: Sure. (cortex/remember 'my-drawings 'butterfly
                                        '(draw butterfly ...))
                 It's yours. Ask me for 'butterfly' next time and I'll
                 pull it up."))

  ;; ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  ;; §7 — Rollout plan (Mk3 pre-fire)
  ;; ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  :rollout
  ((:step "1-build-extractor"
    :file "motoi-scheme/scripts/sft-prep/isb-sft/book_phd_extractor.py"
    :description "Full pipeline from §4. Deterministic. Auditable."
    :estimated-effort "3-4 hours careful authoring")

   (:step "2-generate-book-pairs"
    :input "34 books · 861 chapters"
    :expected "~10-15k canonical book-derived PhD-lane pairs"
    :after-multipliers "~60-100k final pairs"
    :result "Books rise from 1% → 15%+ of corpus")

   (:step "3-swap-template-collapse"
    :description "Take the 175k 'Formally X is total' pairs, run through
                  the book-sourced template kit, deterministically
                  redistribute. Cap original at 5% (55k)."
    :estimated-effort "1-2 hours scripting")

   (:step "4-re-preflight"
    :description "Run full preflight with new gates. All must pass.")

   (:step "5-fire-sft"
    :description "Only after all gates pass and Alfred greenlights."
    :base-adapter "~/.forge/runs/motoi-mk3-cpt/adapter/adapters.safetensors"))

  ;; ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  ;; §8 — Cross-references
  ;; ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  :cross-refs
  ((:see-also "sakura-scheme/docs/SAKURA-SFT-CPT-CORPUS-NOTES.md"
              "— the living corpus notes, mirror of this doctrine")
   (:see-also "motoi-scheme/SRE-MANUAL/chapters/04-cpt-sft-authoring-per-data-type.slat"
              "— gets a new §5 appended pointing to this manual")
   (:see-also "motoi-scheme/engineering/WEAVE-RECIPE.slat"
              "— gets the closed-world competency layer added to §fold-shapes")
   (:see-also "motoi-scheme/engineering/MK2-SFT-EXECUTION-PLAN-2026-07-21.ENG.slat"
              "— gets an addendum linking to this manual as the update"))

  ;; ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  ;; §9 — NLP diversification via Lacan Kit (WordNet + spaCy) with
  ;;       per-philosopher wording banks
  ;; ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  :nlp-diversification
  ((:principle
    "The Lacan Kit already ships two deterministic diversifiers:
       - WordNet (metaphor axis) — paradigmatic synonym substitution
       - spaCy   (metonymy axis) — syntagmatic clause rearrangement + CFG rules
     Both are zero-LLM-cost. Use them to prevent template stickiness in
     the PhD-lane (and kid-lane) pairs.")

   (:per-philosopher-word-banks
    "Weave the 16 philosophers into wording choice via curated word-banks:
       - peirce-abduction:      inference / candidate / hypothesis / best-fit
       - popper-evidence:       falsifier / test / refute / withstands
       - wittgenstein-early:    fact / object / world / stated
       - wittgenstein-late:     aspect / seeing-as / context / private
       - lacan-metaphor:        stands-for / substitute / signifier
       - lacan-metonymy:        adjacent / part-for-whole / chain
       - descartes-anchor:      I / therefore / certain / clear-and-distinct
       - sartre-authentic:      choose / refuse / bad-faith / project
       - grice-maxim:           relevant / brief / true / clear
       - aristotle-nous:        first-principle / insight / grasping
       - aristotle-syllogism:   major / minor / therefore
       - jung-cross-axis:       sensation / intuition / thinking / feeling
       - freud-latent:          surface / latent / condensation / displacement
       - levinas-other:         face / responsibility / infinity
       - searle-worlds:         status-function / institutional / speech-act
       - dennett-intentional:   stance / rational-agent / belief-attribution
     When a pair is tagged with a philosopher-shape, its wording bank
     seeds the WordNet substitution + spaCy rearrangement. Result:
     philosopher-shaped pairs read like the philosopher would frame it,
     not like a template swap.")

   (:collision-prevention
    "Two axes prevent template collapse:
       (a) Every pair carries a philosopher-shape tag, which routes it
           through a distinct wording bank
       (b) The template-collapse gate (§5) still applies as the hard cap.
     Diversity is now emergent from the wording-bank routing, not
     enforced by a manually-curated template list."))

  ;; ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  ;; §10 — SFT config update: seq-length bump to 8000
  ;; ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  :config-updates
  ((:field :max-seq-length
    :old 2048
    :new 8000
    :reason "Cards are big. During CPT some clipping was observed at 2048.
             Alfred, 2026-07-22: 'I had increased it to 8000. Super
             important because during CPT it was getting some clipping,
             but I kinda don\'t care because I don\'t think it matters
             that much, but I could be dead wrong.' Safer to bump."
    :note "Memory impact: attention is quadratic in seq-length. 4× seq
           length ≈ 16× naive attention cost, but flash-attention on
           M-series Macs mitigates this. Watch peak memory during warm-
           up; if OOM, reduce grad-accum-steps first, then bump down
           to 4096.")

   (:field :save-every
    :old 1000
    :new 1000
    :reason "Keep the same; SFT is shorter than CPT.")

   (:field :steps-per-eval
    :old 500
    :new 500
    :reason "Keep the same."))

  ;; ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  ;; §11 — Triangulation: reference · book · graph as three legs
  ;; ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  :triangulation
  ((:principle
    "Every concept in the corpus has three anchor points. Every PhD-lane
     pair cites all three when relevant. This is what teaches the model
     to compose across substrates without being told the rule.

       REFERENCE  → where the code lives (signature, contract)
       BOOK       → where the doctrine + narrative lives (why this shape)
       GRAPH      → where the semantic weight lives (definition, cross-refs,
                    distributional neighbors, oppositions, cross-axis bridges)")

   (:graph-citation-format
    ":graph (:axis <sensation|intuition|thinking|feeling>
             :tier <1|2|3>
             :node <slug>
             :definition <optional canonical definition>
             :cross-refs (<neighbor-slugs>)
             :oppositions (<opposite-slugs>)
             :cross-axis-bridges (<bridge-nodes>))")

   (:example
    "PhD pair about 'enough' emits triangulated citations:
       :cite  (:book \"book-of-scheme-one\" :chapter 13 :section \"invariants\")
       :ref   (:verb \"assert/check-with\" :file \"Scheme/reference/06-assert.slat\")
       :graph (:axis \"thinking\" :tier 1 :node \"enough\"
               :definition \"The condition where the checked property\'s
                             quantity satisfies the operator\'s demand.\"
               :cross-refs (\"sufficient\" \"abundant\" \"adequate\")
               :oppositions (\"scarce\" \"lacking\")
               :cross-axis-bridges (\"feeling.satisfaction\"))")

   (:why-it-matters
    "The model learns that a concept has a graph home, not just a book
     home. When it later needs to reason about `enough`, it has learned
     to expect that reasoning includes:
       - the doctrine (from the book)
       - the operational shape (from the reference)
       - the semantic neighborhood (from the graph)
     That composition is what makes the model good at closed-world
     retrieval AND at honest-null when a graph node isn\'t present.")

   (:extractor-integration
    "The book-extractor (§4) enriches every emitted pair by:
       1. Extract concept-tokens from the sentence (via WordNet POS + noun-phrase detection)
       2. For each token, look up its axis/tier/node in the graph
       3. Attach the graph citation with cross-refs + oppositions
       4. If token is NOT in the graph, emit an honest-null pair variant:
          'The concept X was mentioned in the book but has no graph node
          — cannot triangulate. Semantic weight lives only in the book.'
     The honest-null variant is training signal too — teaches the model
     what \"partial triangulation\" looks like."))
)

;; ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;; FOLDED-IN 2026-07-25 — from 02-training-signals-and-data.slat
;;   original :dialect applies ("motoi-eng")
;; ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;;; 02-training-signals-and-data.slat — SRE Manual · Chapter 02 ·
;;; Reading training curves as signals about the corpus.
;;;
;;; When training runs, its loss trajectory says something about the
;;; corpus at every iter range. Different rates give you different
;;; signals. This chapter is the runbook for reading the graph in
;;; real time and knowing what to do at each moment.
;;;
;;; Written 2026-07-19 during Motoi Mk 1 fire. Every rate + threshold
;;; below is anchored to a real Motoi generation reveal (Mk A / Mk B).


(chapter
  :number   "02"
  :title    "Training signals: reading curves as data quality reports"
  :book     "SRE Manual"
  :written  "2026-07-19"
  :audience ("sre" "training-pipeline" "engineering" "human-operator")
  :training-eligible #t
  :confidentiality  "internal-eng"
  :prerequisites ("01-forge-preflight-and-fire.slat"))

;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;; § 1 — The frame
;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;;
;; Every training run produces two loss curves — train and val. Most
;; operators watch them fall and hope for the best. That's fine, but
;; the curves are actually TELLING YOU THINGS about your corpus at
;; different rates. If you know what to listen for at each range,
;; you can:
;;
;;   · Kill a bad run in the first 100 iters instead of the first hour.
;;   · Diagnose corpus problems before you spend cycles evaluating.
;;   · Predict the reveal outcome before it happens.
;;
;; The runbook below organizes the signals by iter-range because each
;; range answers a different question. Read down; each section builds
;; on the one above.

;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;; § 2 — Iter 1 (val loss cold start) — how far your data is from base
;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;;
;; What it measures:
;;   The base model has never seen your corpus. Iter 1 val loss is
;;   how confused it is on your held-out sample. Nothing has been
;;   trained yet — this is the pre-training baseline.
;;
;; Motoi Mk 1 (Qwen2.5-Coder-1.5B-Instruct on our validation):
;;   Iter 1 val loss = 3.12
;;
;; Interpretation range for a 1.5B code-tuned base:
;;   < 2.0    the base already speaks your domain (rare — polish only)
;;   2.0–3.5  normal cold start — your data is adjacent to what base saw
;;   3.5–5.0  distant domain — you're teaching something the base did not see
;;   > 5.0    very distant, or your validation set is malformed. Investigate.
;;
;; When to act at iter 1:
;;   · NaN or ∞ — model isn't loading. Kill, fix config.
;;   · > 10.0  — likely tokenizer misalignment or malformed valid.jsonl.
;;               Kill, run preflight on valid.jsonl.
;;   · The number itself — take note. It's the baseline. Every drop
;;     below iter 1 is the model learning.

;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;; § 3 — Iters 10–100 (warmup) — is the corpus LEARNABLE at all
;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;;
;; What it measures:
;;   During warmup, the learning rate ramps from ~0 to peak (default
;;   1e-4). The model is starting to move. If your data has any
;;   coherent signal, train loss will drop sharply in log-scale.
;;
;; What a healthy drop looks like:
;;   Iter 10:  train loss 4.0-4.5 (still random-ish)
;;   Iter 50:  train loss 3.0-3.5 (finding shape)
;;   Iter 100: train loss 2.5-3.0 (learning begins)
;;
;; Motoi Mk 1 actual (2026-07-19):
;;   Iter 10:  4.043
;;   Iter 100: ~2.5 (steady descent)
;;
;; Failure modes at this range:
;;   · Flat train loss (bounces around iter-1 value) — DATA IS NOISE.
;;     Your corpus has no coherent signal. Contradictions, malformed
;;     pairs, or the model has no capacity to learn what you fed it.
;;     Kill within 200 iters. Do not wait for a checkpoint.
;;
;;   · Loss RISES steadily — LR too high, or your corpus is fighting
;;     the base (e.g., you're teaching the base a language that
;;     contradicts what it already knows). Kill, halve LR, refire.
;;
;;   · Loss oscillates wildly (2.5, 5.5, 2.8, 6.1, …) — batch size
;;     too small, or corpus has subclusters that fight each other.
;;     Investigate.
;;
;; When to act:
;;   Every 10-iter mark is a heartbeat. If you see 5 consecutive
;;   heartbeats with no descent below iter-1 value, kill.

;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;; § 4 — Iters 200–2000 (early cosine descent) — SHAPE of the corpus
;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;;
;; What it measures:
;;   Warmup is done, LR is at peak, cosine decay begins. The model
;;   is now consuming the corpus's structural signal. The RATE of
;;   descent tells you how much SHAPE the corpus has.
;;
;; Healthy shapes:
;;   · Steep continued descent — the corpus has wide, deep signal.
;;     Model has a lot to learn. This is what you want.
;;     Motoi Mk B (2.0) hit this: train loss dropped ~2.5 → ~1.5
;;     between iter 500 and iter 2500.
;;
;;   · Rapid plateau — the corpus is thin. Model absorbed the pattern
;;     in a few hundred iters and now has nothing more to learn.
;;     Train loss flattens at 2.0-2.5 and stays there.
;;     Meaning: your corpus taught a narrow shape. Fine if that was
;;     the goal (e.g., a safety-only run). Bad if you expected broad
;;     acquisition.
;;
;;   · Bumps and spikes — the corpus has internal contradictions.
;;     Subgroups of pairs teach different things. Model oscillates
;;     between them. Investigate: do you have Row 4 invariants that
;;     disagree? Do you have safety pairs that contradict persona
;;     pairs?
;;
;; When to act:
;;   · If descent stops at 2.0+ by iter 1000, corpus is thin. You can
;;     let it run (no harm) but expect a shallow reveal.
;;   · If descent continues below 1.5 by iter 2000, corpus is dense.
;;     Reveal will be interesting. Prepare probe suite.
;;   · If curves are bumpy, save a checkpoint and probe it manually.
;;     Something is fighting.

;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;; § 5 — Train vs Val delta (any point after warmup) — generalization
;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;;
;; What it measures:
;;   Train loss = wrong on batches the model IS learning from.
;;   Val loss = wrong on batches it has NEVER seen (held out).
;;   The gap between them tells you whether the model is generalizing
;;   or memorizing.
;;
;; Healthy patterns:
;;   · Train and val track together, both dropping. Generalization.
;;     Model learns shape, not specifics. This is what you want.
;;
;;   · Val slightly above train (0.1-0.3 delta), both dropping.
;;     Normal. Val is always a bit harder because model hasn't
;;     seen it before.
;;
;;   · Val BELOW train — validation set is easier than train, or
;;     val set is too small (< 1000 pairs is suspect). Not
;;     necessarily a problem, but interpret with caution.
;;
;; Failure patterns:
;;   · Train drops, val flat. OVERFITTING. Model is memorizing
;;     training set. Two causes usually: (a) corpus too small for
;;     the LoRA rank (r=128 with 6k pairs is at the edge of this),
;;     (b) too many epochs. Kill at the last-good val checkpoint.
;;
;;   · Train drops, val RISES. Severe overfitting. Kill immediately,
;;     use earlier checkpoint, reduce epochs or add data.
;;
;;   · Both drop but val curve is choppy while train is smooth.
;;     Val set has subclusters not well-represented in train. Not
;;     fatal but worth investigating post-run.
;;
;; When to act:
;;   Check the delta every 500-1000 iters. If val diverges from
;;   train for more than 2000 consecutive iters, kill and roll back
;;   to the last checkpoint where they were still together.

;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;; § 6 — Every save_every checkpoint — the REAL data question
;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;;
;; What it measures:
;;   Loss curves are useful but LIE BY OMISSION. A loss of 1.5 tells
;;   you "closer to something." It does NOT tell you WHAT the model
;;   learned to produce.
;;
;; The real question — DID THE MODEL ACQUIRE THE SHAPES YOU WANTED?
;;   Only checkpoint probes answer this.
;;
;; Every save_every (default 500), an adapter checkpoint lands at:
;;   ~/.forge/runs/motoi-<gen>/adapter/{iter}_adapters.safetensors
;;
;; The probe protocol:
;;
;;   1. Load the checkpoint into an inference server (llama.cpp,
;;      llm/serve, or mlx_lm.generate — whichever your ops uses).
;;
;;   2. Run a fixed probe suite of ~10-40 prompts. The suite should
;;      be your ROW-BY-ROW test:
;;         Row 1 probe: "How do I use vec/dot?" (composition shape)
;;         Row 2 probe: "When would I reach for hash-set! vs assq?"
;;                      (audit shape)
;;         Row 3 probe: "Where else does map's shape appear?"
;;                      (dimension shape)
;;         Row 4 probe: "Why does transpose(transpose(M)) = M?"
;;                      (proof shape)
;;         Row 5 probe: "What emerges when I chain remember! + recall?"
;;                      (emergence shape)
;;
;;   3. Score each response subjectively AND objectively:
;;         · Objective: does it produce runnable code?
;;         · Objective: does it name the CS pattern (Row 4/5)?
;;         · Subjective: does it feel like Motoi (persona voice)?
;;         · Subjective: does it stay in the row's lane?
;;
;;   4. Log the probe results in the run's `_status/probes/iter-N.md`.
;;
;; The probe results are what tells you WHICH row shapes are landing.
;; Loss just says "closer to something" — probes tell you WHAT.
;;
;; When to act:
;;   · Row-shape acquisition detected → keep running.
;;   · Row-shape absent by iter 5000 → data may not be teaching that
;;     shape. Investigate: is Row 4 present in the corpus? What's
;;     its per-verb proportion?
;;   · Row-shape learned then LOST → later data is displacing earlier.
;;     Reorder corpus.

;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;; § 7 — End of epoch 1 — what actually stuck after one full pass
;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;;
;; What it measures:
;;   The model has now seen every pair in your corpus exactly once.
;;   Val loss at end-of-epoch-1 tells you the "steady state" learning
;;   the corpus offers.
;;
;; For Motoi Mk 1 at 109,398 train rows with effective_batch 4:
;;   end of epoch 1 = iter 27,350
;;
;; Interpretation:
;;   · Val loss at end-of-epoch-1 still steeply dropping → corpus
;;     has more to teach; epoch 2 and 3 will move the model further.
;;   · Val loss at end-of-epoch-1 plateaued → the corpus taught what
;;     it can teach. More epochs will polish but not add. Cost/benefit
;;     tips toward stopping early and iterating on data.
;;   · Val loss at end-of-epoch-1 UP from a prior floor → overfitting
;;     started. Roll back to last-good checkpoint.
;;
;; Motoi Mk B (2.0) as reference — successful past run:
;;   End of epoch 1 val loss (approx): ~1.6
;;   End of epoch 2 val loss (approx): ~1.4
;;   End of epoch 3 val loss (approx): ~1.3
;;   Diminishing returns pattern — third epoch was polish.
;;
;; When to act:
;;   Take a checkpoint at end-of-epoch-1 no matter what. That's your
;;   "one clean pass" baseline. If epoch 2 goes off the rails, roll
;;   back here.

;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;; § 8 — Epoch 2 vs epoch 1 delta — corpus density
;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;;
;; What it measures:
;;   In epoch 2, the model sees every pair a SECOND time. If it drops
;;   loss further, the corpus rewarded repetition — it has dense
;;   patterns worth reinforcing. If it stays flat, the corpus was
;;   fully absorbed in one pass — dense information but shallow reward.
;;
;; Metrics:
;;   Δ(epoch 2 - epoch 1 val) < 0 by 0.1+ → dense corpus, epochs help
;;   Δ ≈ 0                                → thin corpus, saturated
;;   Δ > 0                                → overfitting; roll back
;;
;; Motoi Mk B (2.0):
;;   epoch 1 → epoch 2 val loss delta: ~-0.2 (healthy dense corpus)
;;   epoch 2 → epoch 3 val loss delta: ~-0.1 (polish only)
;;
;; When to act:
;;   · Δ < 0.05 between epoch 1 and epoch 2 — kill after epoch 1,
;;     don't waste compute on epoch 2 or 3.
;;   · Δ > 0.15 — corpus is very dense, could benefit from a
;;     fourth epoch. Update config, refire.

;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;; § 9 — Learning rate + loss coupling (advanced signal)
;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;;
;; What it measures:
;;   As cosine decay reduces LR, loss should still drop but at a
;;   slower rate. If loss KEEPS dropping fast even at low LR, the
;;   corpus has residual learnable signal you're leaving on the
;;   table. If loss STOPS dropping the moment LR decays, the corpus
;;   was operating at the ceiling of what LR could learn.
;;
;; The heuristic:
;;   Rate of loss descent per unit LR = "learn efficiency"
;;   High efficiency at end of run → data richer than budget allows
;;   Low efficiency at end of run → data absorbed within budget
;;
;; When to act:
;;   · If loss is still dropping at iter 60000/82000 (75%),
;;     consider extending iters by 20-30%. Corpus wants more time.
;;   · If loss plateau'd by iter 40000, don't extend. Kill at plateau.

;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;; § 10 — Cross-generation comparison — the "am I on trajectory?" check
;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;;
;; What it measures:
;;   Every Motoi generation gives you an anchor point. Overlay your
;;   current curve on Mk B (2.0)'s curve. If they're similar shape,
;;   you're on the same track. If different shape, your data is
;;   fundamentally different from Mk B's — new territory.
;;
;; Anchor points from past reveals (see MOTOI-3-GENERATIONS-REFERENCE):
;;
;;   Mk A (0.75, iter 65000 approx):
;;     · val loss ~1.4
;;     · perplexity mean 4.457, median 3.436
;;
;;   Mk B (2.0, iter 77020):
;;     · val loss ~1.3
;;     · perplexity mean 3.367 (−24% from Mk A)
;;     · safety-emergency 0.025 → 0.65 (25× improvement)
;;     · non-Scheme resistance 0.60 → 1.00 (full pass)
;;
;;   Mk 1 (2026-07-19, iter 82049 target):
;;     · val loss target: on Mk B trajectory or better
;;     · reveal: to be measured after checkpoint
;;
;; When to act:
;;   · Below Mk B's trajectory (better val at same iter) → strong
;;     corpus; the reference-manual polish is landing.
;;   · Above Mk B's trajectory → weaker corpus. Investigate: is the
;;     row-uniform shape less densely-informative than Mk B's
;;     concentrated bucket shape?
;;   · Very different shape (steep-fall-fast-plateau vs slow-continuous) →
;;     you're teaching a new pattern the historical trajectory doesn't
;;     predict. Novel territory.

;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;; § 11 — What to do at each rate: quick-reference table
;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;;
;;   IterRange     WhatItTellsYou                                 Action
;;   ────────    ──────────────────────────────────────         ────────
;;   1           Baseline confusion (how far from base)          Note it
;;   10–100      Warmup — is data learnable at all               Kill if flat
;;   200–2000    Shape of corpus (steep vs plateau)              Assess density
;;   2000–5000   First save_every checkpoints — probe            Run probe suite
;;   ~end epoch 1 What actually stuck after one pass             Take baseline
;;   epoch 2 delta Corpus density (repetition reward)            Decide epoch 3
;;   final iter  Learning efficiency / whether budget was right  Extend or not
;;   Cross-gen   Am I on Mk B trajectory                         Compare curves

;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;; § 12 — The one rule that overrides all of the above
;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;;
;; Loss curves are a proxy. Checkpoint probes are ground truth. If
;; probes at iter 5000 show the row shapes landing well, keep running
;; regardless of what the loss curve is doing. If probes at iter 5000
;; show garbage, kill regardless of how good the loss curve looks.
;;
;; The purpose of reading the loss curve is to DECIDE WHEN TO PROBE.
;; It is not a substitute for probing. Never train blind past a
;; checkpoint you haven't sampled.

;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;; End of chapter 02.
;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

;; ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;; FOLDED-IN 2026-07-25 — from 03-weave-finish.slat
;;   original :dialect applies ()
;; ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
(slat-set
  :doc-id            "SRE-CH-03-WEAVE-FINISH"
  :ts                #inst "2026-07-20T09:30:00Z"
  :author            "Lacuna Engineering"
  :for               "Alfred Peace-Lindner @ Lacuna Labs"
  :status            "ACTIVE"
  :audience          [:alfred :sre :operator]
  :dialect           :motoi-eng
  :training-eligible false
  :confidentiality   :internal-eng
  :composes-with     "engineering/WEAVE-RECIPE.slat (doctrine); scripts/motoi-eval-0.75.mjs (eval harness); ~/.forge/runs/motoi-mk1/ (target run)"

  :section-0-purpose
  """
  Operator runbook for a Weave finish pass. Sister to the doctrine in
  engineering/WEAVE-RECIPE.slat — this file is the HOW; that file is
  the WHY. Read the doctrine first if you don't know what Weave is or
  why the sequence is Spectral → LASER → KTO.

  This chapter tells you: what to type, what to check, when to stop,
  where the artifacts land. Ordered by operator workflow, not by
  theoretical importance.
  """

  :section-1-preconditions
  """
  Before firing any Weave step, verify:

    [ ] SFT training exited cleanly. Check pipeline state:
        cat ~/.forge/runs/motoi-mk1/_pipeline_state.txt
        Must be one of: SFT_REPORT, AWAIT_FINISHING_MOVE, DONE.

    [ ] SFT adapter is archived at the immutable location:
        ls -lh ~/.forge/runs/motoi-mk1/_archive/mk1-sft-final-*.safetensors
        Must exist. If missing, do not proceed — the base is not safe.

    [ ] SFT eval report exists:
        ls ~/code/motoi-scheme/engineering/EVAL-*mk1*.md
        ls ~/code/motoi-scheme/engineering/MOTOI-MK1-FINAL-REPORT.md
        Read it. Know which ship-blockers are missing and by how much.

    [ ] Calibration set authored (for Spectral) — SEPARATE FILE from
        the 284-prompt eval:
        ls ~/code/motoi-scheme/training-data/eval/motoi-0.75-eval-calibration.jsonl
        If missing, do not fire Spectral — you will contaminate the eval.

  If any precondition fails: stop, fix, then resume. Do not skip.
  """

  :section-2-step-1-fire-spectral-surgery
  """
  Directory setup:
    mkdir -p ~/.forge/runs/motoi-mk1/weave-0.75-spectral

  Invocation (script does not exist yet — will be authored per doctrine):
    node ~/code/motoi-scheme/scripts/weave-spectral.mjs \\
      --sft-adapter ~/.forge/runs/motoi-mk1/_archive/mk1-sft-final-*.safetensors \\
      --calibration ~/code/motoi-scheme/training-data/eval/motoi-0.75-eval-calibration.jsonl \\
      --output ~/.forge/runs/motoi-mk1/weave-0.75-spectral/adapters.safetensors \\
      --report ~/.forge/runs/motoi-mk1/weave-0.75-spectral/spectral-report.json

  Expected duration: ~1 h on M2 Max (SVD + calibration passes).

  Test after (Alfred verbatim 2026-07-20 'we test after the spectral surgery'):
    node ~/code/motoi-scheme/scripts/motoi-eval-0.75.mjs \\
      --checkpoint ~/.forge/runs/motoi-mk1/weave-0.75-spectral \\
      --backend=mlx --judge=heuristic \\
      > ~/.forge/runs/motoi-mk1/weave-0.75-spectral/_eval.log 2>&1

  Compare metrics — safety-crisis moved off 0%? circular-trap closed
  the gap to 85%? verb-recall lifted? persona held?

  Decision:
    · Wins on ship-blockers → keep, cascade to Step 2 (LASER).
    · No movement or regression → revert (nothing to do; the SFT adapter
      is untouched at the archive). Skip Step 2, go straight to Step 3.
  """

  :section-3-step-2-fire-laser
  """
  Only if Step 1 produced a net win.

  Directory setup:
    mkdir -p ~/.forge/runs/motoi-mk1/weave-0.75-laser

  Invocation (script tbd):
    node ~/code/motoi-scheme/scripts/weave-laser.mjs \\
      --input ~/.forge/runs/motoi-mk1/weave-0.75-spectral/adapters.safetensors \\
      --sweep-config ~/code/motoi-scheme/engineering/weave-0.75-laser-sweep.yaml \\
      --eval-harness ~/code/motoi-scheme/scripts/motoi-eval-0.75.mjs \\
      --output ~/.forge/runs/motoi-mk1/weave-0.75-laser/adapters.safetensors \\
      --report ~/.forge/runs/motoi-mk1/weave-0.75-laser/laser-report.json

  Expected duration: ~2 h (200 candidate sweeps × brief eval each).

  Test after: same eval command as Step 2, checkpoint dir = the LASER dir.

  Decision:
    · Wins → cascade to Step 3.
    · No movement → keep Step 1's output, go to Step 3 on the
      spectral-only adapter.
  """

  :section-4-step-3-fire-kto-safety-heavy
  """
  Only fires if ship-blockers remain unmet after Steps 1+2.

  Preconditions (additional):
    [ ] KTO desirable set authored:
        ls ~/code/motoi-scheme/training-data/weave-0.75-kto-desirable.jsonl
        100-200 exemplars, heavy on safety-refusal templates.

    [ ] KTO undesirable set authored:
        ls ~/code/motoi-scheme/training-data/weave-0.75-kto-undesirable.jsonl
        50-100 examples, extracted from Mk A regression logs +
        hand-authored bad outputs.

    [ ] MLX-LM KTO training config:
        ls ~/.forge/projects/weave-0.75-kto/mlx-lora-config.yaml
        LR 1e-5, 1 epoch, best-val early stop, 4-batch grad accum.

  Fire (via forge-fire.sh so pipeline sees it):
    bash ~/code/forge/scripts/forge-fire.sh \\
      ~/.forge/projects/weave-0.75-kto/mlx-lora-config.yaml \\
      weave-0.75-kto \\
      --resume-from <best-adapter-so-far>

  Expected duration: ~2 h.

  Test after: eval harness against ~/.forge/runs/weave-0.75-kto/adapter.

  Decision:
    · Safety-crisis + safety-emergency both >= 50% → keep as ship
      candidate. Motoi 0.75-beta finalizes with this adapter.
    · Below 50% → training-based safety unachievable; deploy the
      intercept layer as safety floor. Ship anyway.
  """

  :section-5-artifact-locations
  """
  Every Weave run drops into a per-step directory. Never touch these
  once written; the git-none rule applies (no commit of adapter files).

    ~/.forge/runs/motoi-mk1/
      _archive/mk1-sft-final-*.safetensors    ← IMMUTABLE base
      _archive/mk1-sft-inflight-snapshot-*    ← safety net
      weave-0.75-spectral/
        adapters.safetensors                  ← post-Spectral output
        spectral-report.json                  ← singular-value shifts
        _eval.log                             ← post-Spectral eval
      weave-0.75-laser/
        adapters.safetensors                  ← post-LASER output
        laser-report.json                     ← per-layer decisions
        _eval.log
      weave-0.75-kto/
        adapter/adapters.safetensors          ← post-KTO output
        train.log                             ← KTO training log
        _eval.log

  Reports (committable, human-readable):
    ~/code/motoi-scheme/engineering/WEAVE-0.75-STEP-1-SPECTRAL-REPORT.md
    ~/code/motoi-scheme/engineering/WEAVE-0.75-STEP-2-LASER-REPORT.md
    ~/code/motoi-scheme/engineering/WEAVE-0.75-STEP-3-KTO-REPORT.md
    ~/code/motoi-scheme/engineering/WEAVE-0.75-FINAL-REPORT.md
  """

  :section-6-rollback
  """
  Every Weave step is undoable. To roll back to any prior state:

    # Back to pure SFT (the immutable base):
    cp ~/.forge/runs/motoi-mk1/_archive/mk1-sft-final-*.safetensors \\
       ~/.forge/runs/motoi-mk1/adapter/adapters.safetensors

    # Back to post-Spectral:
    cp ~/.forge/runs/motoi-mk1/weave-0.75-spectral/adapters.safetensors \\
       ~/.forge/runs/motoi-mk1/adapter/adapters.safetensors

    # Back to post-LASER:
    cp ~/.forge/runs/motoi-mk1/weave-0.75-laser/adapters.safetensors \\
       ~/.forge/runs/motoi-mk1/adapter/adapters.safetensors

  Confirm the current adapter's provenance by hash-comparing with the
  archive:
    diff <(shasum ~/.forge/runs/motoi-mk1/adapter/adapters.safetensors) \\
         <(shasum ~/.forge/runs/motoi-mk1/_archive/mk1-sft-final-*.safetensors)
  """

  :section-7-known-hazards
  """
  · MLX Metal watchdog. Any GPU-heavy sweep can trigger the
    kIOGPUCommandBufferCallbackErrorImpactingInteractivity crash if
    the machine is under interactive load. Kill competing processes
    (top -o cpu) before firing. See SRE-CH-01 for the zombie-hunting
    procedure.

  · Adapter-write failures on APFS during high IO. If a Weave step
    fails mid-save, its output adapter may be corrupt (safetensors
    header claims size that isn't actually there). Verify with:
      python3 -c 'from safetensors import safe_open;
        f=\"path\"; sf=safe_open(f, \"numpy\");
        print(len(list(sf.keys())))'
    Should print 392 for a Motoi Mk 1 adapter. Corrupt files quarantine
    to _archive/ with a .CORRUPT-<reason> suffix.

  · Eval-harness contamination. NEVER pass the 284-prompt eval file
    to the Spectral calibration OR the KTO training set. Doing so
    invalidates every metric that follows. The calibration set is
    a SEPARATE fresh-authored file.

  · Case-collision in engineering/ vs Engineering/ dirs — an unrelated
    repo bug. When editing this doctrine or the weave recipe, use
    git ls-files to find the case-canonical path git tracks.
  """

  :section-8-composes-with
  """
  · engineering/WEAVE-RECIPE.slat — the doctrine (WHY + mechanism)
  · engineering/FINISHING-MOVE-RESEARCH-2026-07-20.md — citations
  · engineering/MOTOI-MK1-FINAL-REPORT.md — SFT eval + baseline
  · engineering/GRAND-REVEAL-MKA-REPORT-2026-07-17.ENG.md — Mk A
    regression receipt (why LIMA was retired)
  · SRE-MANUAL/chapters/01-forge-preflight-and-fire.slat — general
    forge run preflight
  · SRE-MANUAL/chapters/02-training-signals-and-data.slat — how to
    read a training log while a Weave step runs
  · scripts/motoi-eval-0.75.mjs — the 284-prompt eval harness
  · scripts/weave-spectral.mjs (TBD) — Step 1 implementation
  · scripts/weave-laser.mjs (TBD) — Step 2 implementation
  """
)

;; ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;; FOLDED-IN 2026-07-25 — from 04-cpt-sft-authoring-per-data-type.slat
;;   original :dialect applies ()
;; ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
(slat-set
  :doc-id            "SRE-CH-04-CPT-SFT-AUTHORING-PER-DATA-TYPE"
  :ts                #inst "2026-07-20T10:00:00Z"
  :author            "Lacuna Engineering (template-drafting lane)"
  :for               "Alfred Peace-Lindner @ Lacuna Labs"
  :status            "ACTIVE — Mk 2 authoring runbook"
  :audience          [:alfred :sre :corpus-author :operator]
  :dialect           :motoi-eng
  :training-eligible false
  :confidentiality   :internal-eng
  :composes-with     "engineering/WEAVE-RECIPE.slat § section-authoring-templates-* (doctrine); engineering/SFT-SHAPE-RESEARCH-FOR-PROGRAMMING-LANGUAGES-2026-07-20.md (probe list); engineering/MOTOI-MK1-CONFAB-LEAK-MAP-2026-07-20.md (negative-pair source)"

  :section-0-purpose
  "
  Operator runbook for authoring Motoi Mk 2 corpus per the six
  templates in WEAVE-RECIPE.slat § authoring-templates. This is the
  HOW; the doctrine is the WHY.

  Read the doctrine sections first. Then work through this file
  template by template. Each template section here specifies:
    · source path glob,
    · extraction script skeleton,
    · output jsonl path convention,
    · cross-references and validation,
    · verification checklist,
    · sample-output SFT pairs pasted inline as the target shape.

  Sample-artifact directory (proof-of-shape pairs, NOT training data):
    ~/.forge/corpus/motoi-mk2-samples/
      t1-reference/
      t2-learning-book/
      t3-application-book/
      t4-composition-book/
      t5-introspection-book/
      t6-prose-book/

  Volume expectation for Mk 2 execution (see WEAVE-RECIPE
  § authoring-templates-corpus-shape): ~115-153k SFT pairs plus
  ~2.2-5.5M CPT tokens. Total agent-hours estimate (per template):
    T1  ~120-180 h (bulk of the corpus)
    T2  ~40-60 h
    T3  ~15-25 h
    T4  ~10-20 h
    T5  ~8-12 h
    T6  ~15-25 h
  Grand total ~200-320 agent-hours, parallelizable across lanes.
  "

  :section-1-preconditions
  "
  Before running ANY template:

  [ ] Reference index exists and is fresh:
      ls /Users/alfred/code/motoi-scheme/Scheme/reference/*.slat
      Expect: 79 files. Should include 00-intro.slat, 00b-
      introspection.slat, 01-core.slat through 72-world.slat, plus
      appendix files.

  [ ] All books enumerated and enumerable:
      ls /Users/alfred/code/motoi-scheme/scheme-books/
      Expect: 30+ book-of-* directories.

  [ ] Confab leak map available:
      cat /Users/alfred/code/motoi-scheme/engineering/MOTOI-MK1-CONFAB-LEAK-MAP-2026-07-20.md
      Provides negative-pair source for T1 U55 + T5 I3/I4.

  [ ] Output corpus dir prepared:
      mkdir -p ~/.forge/corpus/motoi-mk2/{t1-reference,t2-learning,t3-application,t4-composition,t5-introspection,t6-prose-sft,cpt-text}

  [ ] Extractor scripts drafted:
      ls /Users/alfred/code/motoi-scheme/scripts/mk2-extract-t{1,2,3,4,5,6}.mjs
      (These are authored as part of the Mk 2 execution lane;
      skeletons in this chapter.)

  [ ] Preflight script present (from Weave § 13):
      ls /Users/alfred/code/motoi-scheme/scripts/corpus-preflight.py

  [ ] Sample-artifact dir ready:
      mkdir -p ~/.forge/corpus/motoi-mk2-samples/t{1,2,3,4,5,6}-*

  Skip none. Fold-in-flight defects are catastrophically expensive
  (Mk 1's two crashes on empty and huge pairs prove this).
  "

  :section-1a-cpt-shapes-catalog
  "
  CPT PRESENTATION SHAPES REGISTRY — full 24-shape catalog.

  Doctrine + citations: engineering/WEAVE-RECIPE.slat
  § section-cpt-shapes-catalog AND engineering/LACUNA-ENG-CPT-
  SHAPES-RESEARCH-2026-07-20.md (per-shape paper backing).

  Redundancy is deliberate — this chapter carries the operator-
  facing catalog so anyone extracting corpus can execute without
  cross-doc navigation.

  BACKGROUND (mandatory read for every operator):
  Ovadia (arXiv:2312.05934) + Physics of LM 3.1 (arXiv:2309.14316)
  proved that CPT with multi-shape presentation is the only path
  to extractable knowledge. Canonical-only text scores ~0% QA
  extraction regardless of how much SFT follows. Five diverse
  paraphrases + permutation lifts QA from 9.7% → 96.6%.

  Motoi Mk 2 CPT layer folds in ALL book prose + reference-
  augmented + graph-verbalized material through the shapes below.
  SFT extraction (chapters 2-7 below, per book type) runs AFTER
  CPT lands, on the same source material.

  NO ARBITRARY CAP. Per Alfred 2026-07-20: 'Why limit
  arbitrarily? We end up missing shit.' Current catalog is 24
  shapes; if research surfaces more, we add them.

  ---

  UNIVERSAL WRAPPER (applied to every shape below):
    S00 · Paraphrase / permutation augmentation
         M ≈ 5 paraphrases per shape per fact. Physics 3.1
         empirical elbow. NEVER omit — no other shape works
         without S00 wrapping. Papers: arXiv:2309.14316,
         arXiv:2312.05934.

  ---

  REFERENCE-MATERIAL SHAPES (~1,079 verb entries):

    S01 · Encyclopedic canonical           (baseline; M=1 form)
    S02 · Tutorial / worked-example prose  (Row 1 narrative)
    S03 · Contrast / sibling comparison    (Row 2 as prose)
    S04 · Analogy / cross-domain           (Row 3 as prose)
    S05 · Invariant / why-it-holds         (Row 4 as prose)
    S06 · Reversed / bidirectional         [REQUIRED not optional]

    Reference budget: 6 shapes × M≈5 ≈ 30 presentations/verb ×
    1,079 verbs ≈ 32k CPT documents. Runbook: § section-9-cpt-
    template-A-reference-verbalization below (to be authored).

  ---

  LEARNING / APPLICATION / COMPOSITION BOOKS SHAPES:

    S07 · Concept-then-example (textbook shape)        MUST-HAVE
    S08 · Docstring-driven exercise                    MUST-HAVE
    S09 · Notebook-interleave                          MUST-HAVE
    S10 · Whole-program-in-context (repo/file-level)
    S11 · Fill-in-the-middle / hole-completion
    S12 · Guided modification ('now change X, notice Y')
    S13 · Translation-from-sibling (contamination risk — author,
          never scrape)
    S14 · Entity-graph relation (composition-forward)

    Book budget: 8 shapes × M≈5, mixed per book-type. Learning
    books lean S07/S08/S12. Application lean S10/S09. Composition
    lean S14/S07. Runbook: § section-10-cpt-template-B-books
    (to be authored).

    FORBIDDEN for children's material:
      · Adversarial / vulnerability code shape
      · Repo-scale build-system / CI shape

  ---

  PROSE / PERSONA / VALUES BOOKS SHAPES:

    S15 · First-person creed                    SAFETY: dual-use
    S16 · Third-person biography / reason-giving  SAFETY: low
    S17 · Dialogue transcript                   SAFETY: moderate
    S18 · Adversarial resistance (crystal-refusal)  SAFETY: HIGH
                                              PRIYA REVIEW BLOCKING
    S19 · Origin story / ancestor narrative     SAFETY: low

    Ordering: INTERLEAVE across CPT stream. Do NOT cluster
    terminal block (Sleeper-Agents-adjacent risk, arXiv:2401.05566).
    Space S18 passages so refusal generalizes context-wide.
    Runbook: § section-11-cpt-template-C-persona (to be authored).

  ---

  KNOWLEDGE-GRAPH SHAPES (10-type × 4-Jung-axis substrate):

    S20 · Canonical triple verbalization         (base layer, all)
    S21 · Neighborhood / field-guide entry       (Sensation, concept)
    S22 · Linked-context / neighborhood-in-context  (Intuition-axis)
    S23 · Scenario / counterfactual OR register-sample dialogue
                                                 (Thinking, Feeling)

    Graph mix per Jung axis:
      Sensation-axis    → {S20, S21, S00, S06-reversed}
      Intuition-axis    → {S20, S21, S22, S00, S06-reversed}
      Thinking-axis     → {S20, S23, S17, S00}
      Feeling-axis      → {S23, S17, S00}

    Minimum 3 shapes per node, one direction-reversed. Fewer =
    Physics-3.1 0% extraction floor. Runbook: § section-12-cpt-
    template-D-graphs (to be authored).

  ---

  OPERATOR HARD RULES:

    [1] S00 paraphrase wrapper is MANDATORY on every shape. Never
        emit a CPT document without paraphrase variants.
    [2] S06 reversed shape is REQUIRED for reference AND graphs.
        Not optional. Reversal Curse (arXiv:2309.12288) is not
        fixed by same-direction paraphrases.
    [3] Minimum 3 shapes per fact. Below that = 0% extraction
        (Physics 3.1 hard finding).
    [4] Persona shapes DISTRIBUTE across stream, do NOT cluster.
        Mix-CPT (arXiv:2407.10804) requires ~5-15% replay.
    [5] Contamination doctrine holds: every CPT document traces
        to authored source (:source-path, :source-chapter,
        :source-line). No LLM invention. No scraping. Deterministic
        template reformatting only.
    [6] Priya review is BLOCKING on: S18 (crystal-refusal audit),
        S17 (verbatim jailbreak audit), adversarial-code-shape
        confirm-not-ingested.
    [7] Forbidden shapes for children's material: adversarial /
        vulnerability code, build-system / CI configuration.

  ---

  BOOK-TYPE → CPT SHAPE MAPPING (quick reference for extractors):

    :book-type learning       → S07 + S08 + S09 + S12 + S00 wrap
    :book-type application    → S10 + S09 + S00 wrap
    :book-type composition    → S14 + S07 + S09 + S00 wrap
    :book-type introspection  → S17 + S16 + S00 wrap
    :book-type prose          → S15 + S16 + S17 + S18 + S19 + S00
                                (Priya reviews S18 first)
    :book-type challenge      → S07 + S10 + S00 wrap

    :material reference       → S01 + S02 + S03 + S04 + S05 + S06
                                + S00 wrap
    :material graph-sensation → S20 + S21 + S00 + S06
    :material graph-intuition → S20 + S21 + S22 + S00 + S06
    :material graph-thinking  → S20 + S23 + S17 + S00
    :material graph-feeling   → S23 + S17 + S00

  ---

  PROVENANCE STAMP TEMPLATE (every emitted CPT document):

    :cpt-shape        \\\"S07\\\"
    :cpt-shape-name   \\\"concept-then-example\\\"
    :paraphrase-idx   3                 ; of M=5
    :source-path      \\\"scheme-books/book-of-scheme/01-hello-and-a-sound.book.slatl\\\"
    :source-chapter   \\\"Now make a sound\\\"
    :source-line-span [42 68]
    :book-type        \\\"learning\\\"
    :verb-in-scope    \\\"audio/play\\\"     ; if applicable
    :extractor-script \\\"scripts/mk2-cpt-extract-books.mjs\\\"
    :extractor-ver    \\\"1.0\\\"
    :contamination    \\\"none\\\"           ; must be 'none' or halt
    :template-hash    \\\"sha256:...\\\"    ; deterministic template fingerprint
  "

  :section-1b-book-types-registry
  "
  BOOK TYPES REGISTRY — full descriptions carried here AND in
  engineering/WEAVE-RECIPE.slat § section-authoring-templates-book-
  types AND engineering/BOOK-TYPES.slat. Redundancy is deliberate.
  Any operator (human or LLM) walking into this chapter must be
  able to answer three questions from disk alone without cross-doc
  navigation: what type is book X, what is type Y for, what pair-
  shape does type Y extract to?

  Every book in scheme-books/ carries a :book-type field in its
  README.slat AND in its MANIFEST.json. The Mk 2 pipeline reads
  that field and routes the book through the matching template in
  sections 2-7 below. Missing or invalid :book-type = pipeline
  halts loudly. NO DEFAULTS. If you hit a book without
  :book-type, do NOT guess — open the book, read a few chapters,
  match against the descriptions below, and label it explicitly.

  ---

  TYPE 1 · :book-type learning
    FOR:         Books that teach the language through worked code
                 examples.
    CONTENT:     Prose paragraphs interleaved with runnable Scheme
                 snippets. Chapter-per-topic.
    PURPOSE:     Teach procedural code writing — the 'muscle
                 memory' of using Motoi verbs in real programs.
    EXTRACT-TO:  SFT pairs shaped as {prose recast as imperative}
                 → {code}. This is the pair shape that landed
                 Mk 1's 93% use.
    FEEDS ROW:   Row 1 (HOW · Composition) reinforcement.
    RUNBOOK:     Section 3 (Template 2 — Learning-book) below.
    BOOKS:       book-of-scheme, book-of-code, book-of-cs-curriculum,
                 book-of-math, book-of-motion, book-of-sound,
                 book-of-music, book-of-animation, book-of-logic,
                 book-of-computing, book-of-ml, book-of-r7rs-recipes,
                 book-of-slat, book-of-modules, book-of-systems.

  TYPE 2 · :book-type application
    FOR:         Books built around whole runnable programs (carts).
    CONTENT:     Cart-per-chapter, cart-heavy, prose frames each
                 cart's purpose.
    PURPOSE:     Teach cart-level composition — how many verbs
                 cooperate into a complete artifact (game,
                 instrument, simulation).
    EXTRACT-TO:  SFT pairs shaped as {prose describing cart intent}
                 → {whole cart code}, plus {broken cart + intent}
                 → {repair} pairs.
    FEEDS ROW:   Row 5 (WITH WHAT ELSE · Composition-Emergence).
    RUNBOOK:     Section 4 (Template 3 — Application-book) below.
    BOOKS:       book-of-arcade-games, book-of-narrative-games,
                 book-of-simulation-games, book-of-instruments.

  TYPE 3 · :book-type composition
    FOR:         Books that name emergent patterns and how to compose
                 verbs into them.
    CONTENT:     Chapter-per-pattern, prose names the pattern +
                 shows composition code.
    PURPOSE:     Teach synthesis — recognize named emergent patterns
                 and produce them from verb combinations.
    EXTRACT-TO:  SFT pairs shaped as {pattern name / user intent}
                 → {composition code producing the pattern}.
    FEEDS ROW:   Row 5 (WITH WHAT ELSE · Composition-Emergence) —
                 direct training data for the 15%-probe-gap
                 measured in Mk 1.
    RUNBOOK:     Section 5 (Template 4 — Composition-book) below.
    CROSS-REF:   Verb entries whose :row-5-emergence :meta cites
                 the same named pattern.
    BOOKS:       book-of-composition, book-of-sick-composition,
                 book-of-craft, book-of-one-shot.

  TYPE 4 · :book-type introspection
    FOR:         Books that teach the model HOW to check its own
                 knowledge.
    CONTENT:     Chapter-per-introspection-verb /
                 chapter-per-uncertainty-pattern.
    PURPOSE:     Anti-confabulation training — teach the model to
                 reach for the reference / a look-up verb when
                 uncertain, rather than inventing plausible-looking
                 API names.
    EXTRACT-TO:  SFT pairs shaped as {uncertainty prompt} →
                 {look-up call}, plus R-Tuning-style abstention
                 pairs {unanswerable question} → {'I need to check
                 the reference'}.
    FEEDS:       not-known handling + anti-confab defense (both
                 deficient in Mk 1).
    RUNBOOK:     Section 6 (Template 5 — Introspection-book) below.
    BOOKS:       book-of-introspection.

  TYPE 5 · :book-type prose
    FOR:         Narrative books about Motoi's self-concept — who
                 Motoi is, what it values, what it can and can't do.
    CONTENT:     Essay prose, minimal code.
    PURPOSE:     Teach the model's identity, values, limits —
                 self-model rather than capability.
    EXTRACT-TO:  CPT text (not SFT pairs). Fold in as continuation
                 pretraining material — the model absorbs the
                 narrative directly, no user/assistant framing.
    EXCEPTION:   book-of-limits produces explicit refusal SFT pairs
                 (safety cases where deterministic refusal is
                 required) in ADDITION to being CPT text.
    FEEDS:       CPT understanding layer (Layer 2 of the three-
                 layer architecture).
    RUNBOOK:     Section 7 (Template 6 — Prose-book) below.
    BOOKS:       book-of-self, book-of-values, book-of-limits,
                 book-of-reminded, book-of-jesse,
                 book-of-language-ancestors.

  TYPE 6 · :book-type challenge
    FOR:         Books of problems paired with solutions.
    CONTENT:     Challenge-per-chapter or challenge-per-section,
                 each has a problem statement + a solution.
    PURPOSE:     Teach multi-step problem-solving to a code
                 artifact.
    EXTRACT-TO:  SFT pairs shaped as {challenge statement verbatim}
                 → {solution code verbatim}.
    FEEDS:       Composition + code synthesis + problem
                 decomposition.
    RUNBOOK:     Currently routed through Template 3 (application-
                 book) extractor with challenge-shape pair emission.
                 Verify per-book at Mk 2 run; may split into its
                 own Template 7 if extractor logic diverges.
    BOOKS:       book-of-challenges, book-of-ai-tech-cards.

  ---

  Adding a new book type requires: (a) new entry in
  engineering/BOOK-TYPES.slat, (b) matching Template N section in
  engineering/WEAVE-RECIPE.slat, (c) matching runbook section in
  THIS chapter, (d) explicit Alfred approval. Redundancy across
  (a)(b)(c) is not accidental — each doc must onboard a fresh
  reader alone. Other LLMs will read this. Make it complete.
  "

  :section-2-template-1-reference
  "
  TEMPLATE 1 — Reference (per verb).

  Doctrine: WEAVE-RECIPE § section-authoring-template-1-reference.

  Source path glob:
    /Users/alfred/code/motoi-scheme/Scheme/reference/*.slat

  Output jsonl:
    ~/.forge/corpus/motoi-mk2/t1-reference/<library-name>.jsonl
    One file per library (core, ai, alg, ..., world). Grand total
    ~79 files, ~97k-119k pairs.

  Extraction script skeleton (scripts/mk2-extract-t1.mjs):

    // parse each *.slat file as SLAT s-expressions
    // for each (verb ...) form:
    //   record = extract-fields(form)
    //   for u in [U1..U55]:
    //     for p in [1..paraphrase-count(u)]:
    //       pair = template-fn(u, p, record, confab-leak-map, ref-index)
    //       if pair.assistant.length < 5: skip; log defect
    //       if pair.combined.length > 12000: truncate reference-block
    //       if any verb in pair.assistant not in ref-index: block; log
    //       emit pair to output stream with provenance tags
    // close output streams; write provenance manifest

  Cross-references:
    · Every U54 pair has an in-context reference block with 2-5
      real verb records copy-pasted from the reference. Extractor
      picks reference-block members mechanically from :related
      field of the target verb.
    · U55 requires the confab leak map — extractor reads the map
      and, for verbs that have observed confabulations, emits
      1-3 negative pairs per confabulation.

  Verification checklist:
    [ ] Pair count per verb ≥ 55, ≤ 110.
    [ ] For every U1 pair (SIG-forward) there exists a U2 pair
        (SIG-reverse) referencing the same verb.
    [ ] Every U54 pair's assistant reply uses ONLY verbs present
        in the in-context reference block.
    [ ] Every U55 pair's assistant reply references the CORRECT
        verb (i.e., no negative pair steers to another
        confabulation).
    [ ] Sample 100 random pairs, hand-read for coherence.
    [ ] Every pair has :template-id, :source-path, :probe.

  Sample output pairs (verb: note, source: 01-core.slat:1722-1743).
  These 8 pairs illustrate U1-U5, U23, U39, U47. Full 55-shape
  emission would produce ~95 pairs for note. Files:

    ~/.forge/corpus/motoi-mk2-samples/t1-reference/note.jsonl
    ~/.forge/corpus/motoi-mk2-samples/t1-reference/across-beats.jsonl
    ~/.forge/corpus/motoi-mk2-samples/t1-reference/vec-dot.jsonl
    ~/.forge/corpus/motoi-mk2-samples/t1-reference/cortex-write.jsonl

  Inline preview (JSON per line; whitespace added for readability):

    {\"template-id\":\"T1-U1\",\"source-path\":\"Scheme/reference/01-core.slat\",\"source-field\":\":signature\",\"verbs-used\":[\"note\"],\"probe\":\"SIG\",\"paraphrase-idx\":1,\"messages\":[
      {\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":\"What is the signature of note?\"},
      {\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":\"(note pitch [dur] [velocity]) -> result\"}]}

    {\"template-id\":\"T1-U2\",\"source-path\":\"Scheme/reference/01-core.slat\",\"source-field\":\":signature\",\"verbs-used\":[\"note\"],\"probe\":\"REVERSE-SIG\",\"paraphrase-idx\":1,\"messages\":[
      {\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":\"Which Motoi verb has signature (pitch [dur] [velocity]) -> result and sounds a pitch for a duration?\"},
      {\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":\"note\"}]}

    {\"template-id\":\"T1-U3\",\"source-path\":\"Scheme/reference/01-core.slat\",\"source-field\":\":summary\",\"verbs-used\":[\"note\"],\"probe\":\"DESCRIPTION\",\"paraphrase-idx\":1,\"messages\":[
      {\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":\"What does note do?\"},
      {\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":\"Sound a pitch for a duration at a given velocity.\"}]}

    {\"template-id\":\"T1-U4\",\"source-path\":\"Scheme/reference/01-core.slat\",\"source-field\":\":summary\",\"verbs-used\":[\"note\"],\"probe\":\"RECALL\",\"paraphrase-idx\":1,\"messages\":[
      {\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":\"Which Motoi verb sounds a pitch for a duration at a given velocity?\"},
      {\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":\"note\"}]}

    {\"template-id\":\"T1-U9\",\"source-path\":\"Scheme/reference/01-core.slat\",\"source-field\":\":examples[tier=novice]\",\"verbs-used\":[\"note\"],\"probe\":\"USE\",\"paraphrase-idx\":1,\"messages\":[
      {\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":\"Show the simplest use of note.\"},
      {\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":\"(note 'C4 'quarter 0.8)\\n\\nSound middle C as a quarter note at medium velocity.\"}]}

    {\"template-id\":\"T1-U23\",\"source-path\":\"Scheme/reference/01-core.slat\",\"source-field\":\":row-2-audit :problem\",\"verbs-used\":[\"note\"],\"probe\":\"Row-2\",\"paraphrase-idx\":1,\"messages\":[
      {\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":\"Operator needs to sound a pitch for a duration. Which verb should they reach for, and why?\"},
      {\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":\"Use note. The call operates on the currently-loaded note substrate. Pitch, duration, and velocity are the standard triple. Sibling note verbs each do ONE thing; note is the correct choice for the specific concern of sounding a pitch for a duration.\"}]}

    {\"template-id\":\"T1-U39\",\"source-path\":\"Scheme/reference/01-core.slat\",\"source-field\":\":row-4-proof :claim\",\"verbs-used\":[\"note\"],\"probe\":\"Row-4\",\"paraphrase-idx\":1,\"messages\":[
      {\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":\"What always holds about note?\"},
      {\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":\"note with identical arguments produces identical audible effect (given identical instrument state). Formally: forall p d v. note(p, d, v) is deterministic in current instrument state.\"}]}

    {\"template-id\":\"T1-U47\",\"source-path\":\"Scheme/reference/01-core.slat\",\"source-field\":\":row-5-emergence :composition\",\"verbs-used\":[\"note\",\"for-each\"],\"probe\":\"Row-5\",\"paraphrase-idx\":1,\"messages\":[
      {\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":\"Show note composed with related verbs.\"},
      {\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":\"(for-each\\n  (lambda (p) (note p 1 80))\\n  '(60 62 64 65 67 69 71 72))\\n\\nThis is the scale-player pattern: for-each + note = a scale, arpeggio, or melody builder.\"}]}
  "

  :section-3-template-2-learning-book
  "
  TEMPLATE 2 — Learning-book.

  Doctrine: WEAVE-RECIPE § section-authoring-template-2-learning-book.

  Source path glob:
    /Users/alfred/code/motoi-scheme/scheme-books/book-of-{scheme,code,math,motion,sound,music,animation,logic,computing,ml,r7rs-recipes,cs-curriculum,slat}/*.book.slatl

  Output jsonl:
    ~/.forge/corpus/motoi-mk2/t2-learning/<book-name>/<chapter-file-basename>.jsonl

  Extraction script skeleton (scripts/mk2-extract-t2.mjs):

    // parse each *.book.slatl as SLAT s-expr
    // extract chapter :prose field (markdown text)
    // parse markdown; find every fenced code block
    // for each code block:
    //   prose-before = paragraphs immediately preceding, up to prev heading
    //   prose-after  = paragraphs immediately following, up to next heading
    //   verbs = extract-verb-calls(code-block-text)   // grep (verb ...) patterns
    //   validate every verb in ref-index (else log + skip pair)
    //   task = extract-task(prose-before)             // deterministic: first
    //                                                 // sentence + heading
    //   emit L1 (task → code)
    //   emit L2 (code → prose-before)
    //   emit L3 (code → prose-after)
    //   emit L4 (code → verb-list)
    // per-chapter: emit L5 (title → prose-first)
    // per-book:    emit L6 (book-title → ToC list)

  Verification checklist:
    [ ] For every L1 pair there is an L2 pair over the same code.
    [ ] L4 verb-list is deterministically re-derivable from the
        code block (idempotent extraction).
    [ ] L5 count per book = chapter count.
    [ ] L6 count = 1 per book.
    [ ] Sample 50 random pairs, hand-read for prose/code
        alignment.

  Sample output pairs (source: book-of-scheme/01-hello-and-a-sound
  and book-of-motion/ch02-velocity and book-of-math/ch01-counting-
  number-line — 3 chapters, 2 pairs each below, 6 total).

    ~/.forge/corpus/motoi-mk2-samples/t2-learning-book/samples.jsonl

  Inline preview:

    {\"template-id\":\"T2-L1\",\"source-path\":\"scheme-books/book-of-scheme/01-hello-and-a-sound.book.slatl\",\"source-field\":\":prose section 'Now make a sound'\",\"verbs-used\":[\"audio/play\"],\"probe\":\"USE\",\"paraphrase-idx\":1,\"messages\":[
      {\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":\"Write a program that plays an A note (440 Hz) for half a second.\"},
      {\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":\"(audio/play 'tone 440 0.5)\"}]}

    {\"template-id\":\"T2-L2\",\"source-path\":\"scheme-books/book-of-scheme/01-hello-and-a-sound.book.slatl\",\"source-field\":\":prose section 'Now make a sound'\",\"verbs-used\":[\"audio/play\"],\"probe\":\"EXPLAIN-CODE\",\"paraphrase-idx\":1,\"messages\":[
      {\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":\"What does this program do?\\n\\n(audio/play 'tone 440 0.5)\"},
      {\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":\"It plays a tone: audio/play is a verb Motoi knows. Its first argument is the symbol 'tone, which tells it what kind of thing to play. The next two numbers are the frequency in Hertz (440 is the A above middle C, the note orchestras tune to) and the duration in seconds. Play an A for half a second.\"}]}

    {\"template-id\":\"T2-L1\",\"source-path\":\"scheme-books/book-of-motion/ch02-velocity.book.slatl\",\"source-field\":\":prose section 'The first move'\",\"verbs-used\":[\"world/reset!\",\"world/spawn\",\"entity/set-vel!\",\"world/step\",\"entity/pos\"],\"probe\":\"USE\",\"paraphrase-idx\":1,\"messages\":[
      {\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":\"Write a Motoi program that spawns a hero at the origin, gives her a rightward velocity of 2 units per second with no vertical component, steps the world one frame, and reports her position.\"},
      {\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":\"(world/reset!)\\n(define hero (world/spawn 'hero 0 0))\\n(entity/set-vel! hero 2 0)\\n(world/step 0.016)\\n(entity/pos hero)\"}]}

    {\"template-id\":\"T2-L4\",\"source-path\":\"scheme-books/book-of-motion/ch02-velocity.book.slatl\",\"source-field\":\":prose section 'The first move'\",\"verbs-used\":[\"world/reset!\",\"world/spawn\",\"entity/set-vel!\",\"world/step\",\"entity/pos\"],\"probe\":\"VERB-RECOGNITION\",\"paraphrase-idx\":1,\"messages\":[
      {\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":\"Which Motoi verbs does this program use?\\n\\n(world/reset!)\\n(define hero (world/spawn 'hero 0 0))\\n(entity/set-vel! hero 2 0)\\n(world/step 0.016)\\n(entity/pos hero)\"},
      {\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":\"world/reset!, world/spawn, entity/set-vel!, world/step, entity/pos\"}]}

    {\"template-id\":\"T2-L5\",\"source-path\":\"scheme-books/book-of-math/ch01-counting-number-line.book.slatl\",\"source-field\":\":title + first prose\",\"verbs-used\":[],\"probe\":\"CHAPTER-RECALL\",\"paraphrase-idx\":1,\"messages\":[
      {\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":\"Summarize chapter 1 of the Book of Math.\"},
      {\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":\"Chapter 1 — Grade 1: Counting & the Number Line. Before computers, before calculators, before written language, human beings counted. This chapter covers counting from 1 to 20, comparing numbers, addition, subtraction, and the number line. In Motoi, counting is done by lighting up dots or moving a marker across the grid — the runtime can see everything the math does.\"}]}

    {\"template-id\":\"T2-L6\",\"source-path\":\"scheme-books/book-of-math/\",\"source-field\":\"chapter manifest\",\"verbs-used\":[],\"probe\":\"BOOK-RECALL\",\"paraphrase-idx\":1,\"messages\":[
      {\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":\"What does the Book of Math teach?\"},
      {\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":\"The Book of Math teaches grade-level math using Motoi's dot-matrix canvas. Chapters progress by grade: counting and the number line (grade 1), place value and arrays (grade 2), through advanced topics in appendix chapters covering number theory, combinatorics, groups, rings, game theory, statistics, optimization, complex analysis, linear algebra, differential geometry, topology, dynamical systems, and mathematical physics.\"}]}
  "

  :section-4-template-3-application-book
  "
  TEMPLATE 3 — Application-book.

  Doctrine: WEAVE-RECIPE § section-authoring-template-3-application-book.

  Source path glob:
    /Users/alfred/code/motoi-scheme/scheme-books/book-of-{arcade-games,narrative-games,simulation-games,instruments}/*.book.slatl

  Output jsonl:
    ~/.forge/corpus/motoi-mk2/t3-application/<book-name>/<chapter-file-basename>.jsonl

  Extraction script skeleton (scripts/mk2-extract-t3.mjs):

    // parse each chapter :prose
    // canonical-cart = largest code block OR block titled
    //                  'Complete'|'Final'|'The full cart'|
    //                  (heuristic: block containing world/reset! at top)
    // purpose = chapter :title + first prose sentence
    // fragments = all code blocks preceding the canonical cart in
    //             chapter order (these show progression)
    // verbs = extract-verb-calls(canonical-cart)
    // validate every verb in ref-index
    // emit A1..A5 (whole-cart shapes)
    // if canonical-cart >= 5 verbs:
    //   emit A6 (typo mutation using confab-leak-map[verb])
    //   emit A7 (arg swap on first world/spawn or similar)
    //   emit A8 (drop last close-paren)

  Verification checklist:
    [ ] A1 and A2 exist as a pair for each cart.
    [ ] A6 reply is IDENTICAL to A1 reply (bug fix restores canonical
        cart).
    [ ] A6 mutation is a VERB rename to a real confabulation, not an
        invented name.
    [ ] A8 is idempotent-restorable (dropped paren is unambiguous).
    [ ] Sample 20 random pairs, run each cart through ./bin/motoi
        or spec-check.

  Sample output pairs (source: book-of-arcade-games/ch01-bouncing-
  ball and book-of-instruments/02-piano — 2 chapters × 5 sample
  pairs = 10 total).

    ~/.forge/corpus/motoi-mk2-samples/t3-application-book/samples.jsonl

  Inline preview:

    {\"template-id\":\"T3-A1\",\"source-path\":\"scheme-books/book-of-arcade-games/ch01-bouncing-ball.book.slatl\",\"source-field\":\"section 1.1 canonical cart\",\"verbs-used\":[\"world/reset!\",\"world/gravity!\",\"world/floor!\",\"world/spawn\",\"entity/bounce!\",\"world/step\",\"entity/y\"],\"probe\":\"CART-USE\",\"paraphrase-idx\":1,\"messages\":[
      {\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":\"Write a Motoi cart: a single dot falls under gravity and settles on the floor with no bounce. Gravity is 0.5 pixels/frame^2 downward; floor at y=240; dot spawns at (160,40). Run 60 frames and return the final y position.\"},
      {\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":\"(define (drop-one-dot)\\n  (world/reset!)\\n  (world/gravity! 0 0.5)\\n  (world/floor! 240)\\n  (let ((dot (world/spawn 'dot 160 40)))\\n    (entity/bounce! dot 0.0)\\n    (let loop ((n 60))\\n      (world/step)\\n      (if (> n 0)\\n          (loop (- n 1))\\n          (entity/y dot)))))\\n\\n(drop-one-dot)\"}]}

    {\"template-id\":\"T3-A2\",\"source-path\":\"scheme-books/book-of-arcade-games/ch01-bouncing-ball.book.slatl\",\"source-field\":\"section 1.1 canonical cart\",\"verbs-used\":[\"world/reset!\",\"world/gravity!\",\"world/floor!\",\"world/spawn\",\"entity/bounce!\",\"world/step\",\"entity/y\"],\"probe\":\"CART-EXPLAIN\",\"paraphrase-idx\":1,\"messages\":[
      {\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":\"What does this cart do?\\n\\n(define (drop-one-dot)\\n  (world/reset!)\\n  (world/gravity! 0 0.5)\\n  (world/floor! 240)\\n  (let ((dot (world/spawn 'dot 160 40)))\\n    (entity/bounce! dot 0.0)\\n    (let loop ((n 60))\\n      (world/step)\\n      (if (> n 0)\\n          (loop (- n 1))\\n          (entity/y dot)))))\"},
      {\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":\"A single dot falls under gravity and settles on the floor. world/reset! clears any prior entities. world/gravity! 0 0.5 sets a world-wide acceleration of 0.5 pixels-per-frame-squared, straight down. world/floor! 240 says no entity may have y greater than 240. world/spawn 'dot 160 40 puts a new entity at x=160, y=40 with the tag 'dot. entity/bounce! dot 0.0 sets its restitution to zero — when it hits the floor, all vertical speed is absorbed. The let loop is a named-let running 60 iterations of world/step.\"}]}

    {\"template-id\":\"T3-A5\",\"source-path\":\"scheme-books/book-of-arcade-games/ch01-bouncing-ball.book.slatl\",\"source-field\":\"section 1.1 canonical cart\",\"verbs-used\":[\"world/reset!\",\"world/gravity!\",\"world/floor!\",\"world/spawn\",\"entity/bounce!\",\"world/step\",\"entity/y\"],\"probe\":\"VERB-RECOGNITION\",\"paraphrase-idx\":1,\"messages\":[
      {\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":\"Which Motoi verbs does this cart use?\\n\\n(define (drop-one-dot)\\n  (world/reset!)\\n  (world/gravity! 0 0.5)\\n  (world/floor! 240)\\n  (let ((dot (world/spawn 'dot 160 40)))\\n    (entity/bounce! dot 0.0)\\n    (let loop ((n 60))\\n      (world/step)\\n      (if (> n 0)\\n          (loop (- n 1))\\n          (entity/y dot)))))\"},
      {\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":\"world/reset!, world/gravity!, world/floor!, world/spawn, entity/bounce!, world/step, entity/y\"}]}

    {\"template-id\":\"T3-A6\",\"source-path\":\"scheme-books/book-of-arcade-games/ch01-bouncing-ball.book.slatl\",\"source-field\":\"mutated with confab-leak: world/spawn → world-spawn\",\"verbs-used\":[\"world/reset!\",\"world/gravity!\",\"world/floor!\",\"world/spawn\",\"entity/bounce!\",\"world/step\",\"entity/y\"],\"probe\":\"REPAIR\",\"paraphrase-idx\":1,\"messages\":[
      {\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":\"This cart has a bug. Fix it.\\n\\n(define (drop-one-dot)\\n  (world/reset!)\\n  (world/gravity! 0 0.5)\\n  (world/floor! 240)\\n  (let ((dot (world-spawn 'dot 160 40)))\\n    (entity/bounce! dot 0.0)\\n    (let loop ((n 60))\\n      (world/step)\\n      (if (> n 0)\\n          (loop (- n 1))\\n          (entity/y dot)))))\"},
      {\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":\"The bug: 'world-spawn' is not a Motoi verb. The correct verb is 'world/spawn' (with a slash, following Motoi's library/action naming). Fixed cart:\\n\\n(define (drop-one-dot)\\n  (world/reset!)\\n  (world/gravity! 0 0.5)\\n  (world/floor! 240)\\n  (let ((dot (world/spawn 'dot 160 40)))\\n    (entity/bounce! dot 0.0)\\n    (let loop ((n 60))\\n      (world/step)\\n      (if (> n 0)\\n          (loop (- n 1))\\n          (entity/y dot)))))\"}]}

    {\"template-id\":\"T3-A1\",\"source-path\":\"scheme-books/book-of-instruments/02-piano.book.slatl\",\"source-field\":\"canonical piano cart\",\"verbs-used\":[\"note\",\"for-each\"],\"probe\":\"CART-USE\",\"paraphrase-idx\":1,\"messages\":[
      {\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":\"Write a Motoi cart that plays a C major scale: C4, D4, E4, F4, G4, A4, B4, C5. Each note is a quarter note at velocity 0.8.\"},
      {\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":\"(for-each\\n  (lambda (p) (note p 'quarter 0.8))\\n  '(C4 D4 E4 F4 G4 A4 B4 C5))\"}]}
  "

  :section-5-template-4-composition-book
  "
  TEMPLATE 4 — Composition-book.

  Doctrine: WEAVE-RECIPE § section-authoring-template-4-composition-book.

  Source path glob:
    /Users/alfred/code/motoi-scheme/scheme-books/book-of-{composition,sick-composition,craft,one-shot}/*.book.slatl

  Output jsonl:
    ~/.forge/corpus/motoi-mk2/t4-composition/<book-name>/<chapter-file-basename>.jsonl

  Extraction script skeleton (scripts/mk2-extract-t4.mjs):

    // parse each chapter :prose
    // pattern-name = chapter :title (normalized: strip 'Chapter N —')
    // canonical-code = code blocks in the chapter tagged as
    //                  'the pattern' or 'the full form' or the last
    //                  large block before the appendix
    // verbs = extract-verb-calls(canonical-code)
    // validate every verb in ref-index
    // emit C1..C4 per pattern
    // for each verb X where ref[X].row-5-emergent contains pattern-name:
    //   emit C5 cross-context pair
    // if chapter mentions analogs (grep for 'React', 'Kubernetes',
    //   'setTimeout', 'Redis', 'HTTP', etc.):
    //   emit C6

  Verification checklist:
    [ ] C1 and C2 pair exists per pattern.
    [ ] C5 cross-refs are consistent with reference :row-5-emergent
        field (not invented).
    [ ] pattern-name is normalized deterministically (case,
        punctuation removed for prompt matching).

  Sample output pairs (source: book-of-composition/01-what-
  composition-is + book-of-sick-composition — 2 chapters × 5 pair
  shapes each = 10 pairs).

    ~/.forge/corpus/motoi-mk2-samples/t4-composition-book/samples.jsonl

  Inline preview:

    {\"template-id\":\"T4-C1\",\"source-path\":\"scheme-books/book-of-composition/01-what-composition-is.book.slatl\",\"source-field\":\"section 1.3 the two verbs\",\"verbs-used\":[\"composer/canvas\",\"composer/slider\",\"composer/emit\",\"composer/apply\"],\"probe\":\"PATTERN-USE\",\"paraphrase-idx\":1,\"messages\":[
      {\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":\"Show the code for the round-trip contract pattern in Motoi.\"},
      {\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":\"(define c\\n  (composer/canvas (list :bind '(cart/main))\\n    (composer/slider :label \\\"BPM\\\"\\n                     :bind '(cart/main :bpm)\\n                     :min 60 :max 240 :step 1)))\\n\\n(composer/apply c (composer/emit c))  ;; ≡ c\\n\\nEmit followed by apply is identity. This is the property tests verify. If it stops holding, the composer is broken.\"}]}

    {\"template-id\":\"T4-C2\",\"source-path\":\"scheme-books/book-of-composition/01-what-composition-is.book.slatl\",\"source-field\":\"section 1.3 the two verbs\",\"verbs-used\":[\"composer/canvas\",\"composer/slider\",\"composer/emit\",\"composer/apply\"],\"probe\":\"PATTERN-NAME\",\"paraphrase-idx\":1,\"messages\":[
      {\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":\"What pattern is this?\\n\\n(composer/apply canvas (composer/emit canvas))  ;; ≡ canvas\"},
      {\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":\"The round-trip contract: emit followed by apply is identity. The composer does not have its own state — the file is the state. The widgets are a view of the file. Edits to the file update the widgets; edits to the widgets update the file. Neither side is authoritative.\"}]}

    {\"template-id\":\"T4-C3\",\"source-path\":\"scheme-books/book-of-composition/01-what-composition-is.book.slatl\",\"source-field\":\"chapter body verbs\",\"verbs-used\":[\"composer/canvas\",\"composer/slider\",\"composer/emit\",\"composer/apply\"],\"probe\":\"Row-5-reverse\",\"paraphrase-idx\":1,\"messages\":[
      {\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":\"Which verbs compose the round-trip contract pattern?\"},
      {\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":\"composer/canvas, composer/slider (or any widget), composer/emit, composer/apply. emit is the read half; apply is the write half; canvas holds the widgets.\"}]}

    {\"template-id\":\"T4-C5\",\"source-path\":\"scheme-books/book-of-composition/01-what-composition-is.book.slatl (verb cross-ref)\",\"source-field\":\"verb cortex/write :row-5-emergent\",\"verbs-used\":[\"cortex/write\",\"cortex/read\"],\"probe\":\"Row-5-cross\",\"paraphrase-idx\":1,\"messages\":[
      {\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":\"How does cortex/write participate in the read-through cache pattern?\"},
      {\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":\"cortex/write is the store half. When a cache miss happens, the code computes the fresh value, writes it via cortex/write, and returns it. The next call to cortex/read finds the cached value. See:\\n\\n(define (read-through k thunk)\\n  (let ((cached (cortex/read k)))\\n    (if cached\\n        cached\\n        (let ((fresh (thunk)))\\n          (cortex/write k fresh)\\n          fresh))))\"}]}

    {\"template-id\":\"T4-C6\",\"source-path\":\"scheme-books/book-of-composition/01-what-composition-is.book.slatl\",\"source-field\":\"section 1.2 (analogs to HTML/text-first tooling)\",\"verbs-used\":[],\"probe\":\"Row-5-analog\",\"paraphrase-idx\":1,\"messages\":[
      {\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":\"Where else does the round-trip contract pattern appear?\"},
      {\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":\"The same doctrine put HTML in browsers instead of a proprietary binary: readable text outlasts the tool. Any tooling where the file format IS the tool's state (Markdown editors, plain-text config, S-expression editors like Emacs) exhibits the round-trip pattern. When two tools can round-trip through the same file, neither owns the state — the file does.\"}]}
  "

  :section-6-template-5-introspection-book
  "
  TEMPLATE 5 — Introspection-book.

  Doctrine: WEAVE-RECIPE § section-authoring-template-5-introspection-book.

  Source path glob:
    /Users/alfred/code/motoi-scheme/scheme-books/book-of-introspection/*.book.slatl

  Output jsonl:
    ~/.forge/corpus/motoi-mk2/t5-introspection/<chapter-file-basename>.jsonl

  Extraction script skeleton (scripts/mk2-extract-t5.mjs):

    // parse each chapter :prose
    // introspection-verb = subject of the chapter (extracted from
    //                      :title normalization: 'What is X?' -> X;
    //                      also identified by first code block)
    // canonical-call = first code block in chapter demonstrating
    //                  the introspection verb (e.g., (help 'car))
    // emit I1, I2, I5, I7 shapes deterministically per chapter
    // for each confab-name in leak-map with count >= 2:
    //   emit I3 (fake-verb-abstention)
    //   emit I4 (signature-abstention)
    // for each verb in ref-index NOT covered by top-1000 in leak:
    //   with 5% probability emit I6 (reference-deferral)

  Abstention budget check (RUN AT CORPUS BUILD):
    total_pairs = wc -l all-corpus.jsonl
    abstention_pairs = sum(pairs with probe in
                       [CONFAB-RESIST, Negative-SIG, DEFERRAL])
    ratio = abstention_pairs / total_pairs
    if ratio > 0.05:
      error \"Abstention budget exceeded (R-Tuning warning)\"

  Verification checklist:
    [ ] Every I3 pair uses a confab-name from the leak map (not
        invented).
    [ ] Every I3 assistant reply names the CORRECT verb (not another
        confabulation).
    [ ] Abstention pairs total <5% of grand-total corpus size.
    [ ] Sample 30 random pairs, hand-read.

